(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F3 / U52 / U99 / G9 / G13: WHAT THE 2026-10-29 RULING DID. The operative order (Dkt 148) does three things. (a) It declares unlawful, vacates and sets aside under 5 U.S.C. 706(2) only the IFR words requiring that a frail person's condition \"significantly impairs\" their ability to comply. The vacatur is NOT limited to the plaintiff states on its face. The order is silent on \"nationwide\"; that label comes from press (HFMA). (b) It enjoins CMS from applying that provision to the PLAINTIFF STATES. (c) It enjoins CMS from penalizing the PLAINTIFF STATES for errors or delays in implementing the IFR for six months from 2026-10-29, i.e. to about 2027-04-29. CORRECTED: the reports' \"six-month stay of enforcement\" is not a stay of the ruling and not a delay of the work requirement. It is a six-month bar on CMS penalizing the 25 suing states plus DC. The opinion frames it around frailty determinations, but the order's text covers \"errors or delays in implementing the IFR\". Error source: secondary relay (KSAT, CA AG release, HFMA). Safe wording: \"A federal judge struck the 'significantly impairs' test out of the rule and barred CMS from enforcing it against the 25 states and DC that sued.\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F3 / U52: THE HOLDING. The court held that CMS had no authority to carve any subset out of the five frailty groups Congress listed, which makes the added test ultra vires and contrary to law. Having decided that, it did not reach the arbitrary-and-capricious or Spending Clause arguments on the frailty definition.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F3 / F4 / U52 / U99: UPHELD. The 12-month claims look-back for verifying frailty and the one-sworn-statement-per-enrollment-period limit both survived. The court found the states' objections fell short of the arbitrary-and-capricious standard. These two IFR provisions remain operative everywhere.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F3: the IFR preamble's protection, quoted by the court: a missing claim, or a missing type of claim, may not by itself be used to deny the frailty exclusion.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F3 / G9: THE WORK REQUIREMENT ITSELF STANDS. The 2027-01-01 start date is untouched. At the 10-22 hearing the states said they were not asking to move it, and the court said it could not have done so anyway.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G1(b) / G13: FINAL JUDGMENT and APPEAL STATUS. Judgment entered 2026-10-29 (Dkt 149) and the case was dismissed. As of 2026-11-02 no notice of appeal and no CMS guidance appeared in the tracker's filing list or the press checked; reports say CMS \"did not immediately\" say whether it would appeal. Say \"the government has not said whether it will appeal (as of Nov 2)\". Re-check before air.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U68: the states lost their preliminary-injunction bid in July (order of 2026-07-29), partly because the court put the timeline on Congress, not CMS. Filing date 2026-06-29 per the tracker and Dkt 3. The plaintiffs are 25 states plus DC; Kentucky and Pennsylvania sue through their governors. U68's \"the court indicated full briefing before Jan 1\" was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U99: in June the suing states asked CMS for a six-month extension of the 2027-01-01 deadline. CMS's answer is not in any filing I read. By October the states told the court they were not seeking an extension (A5).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U53 / G9 / G1(c): Taylor v. Kennedy (D. Md., GLR-26-3705). The government's cross-motion is due 2026-11-03. ADDED and load-bearing: the court REFUSED to promise a ruling before 2027-01-01, and briefing runs to 2026-12-02. So no Maryland ruling should be expected before the requirement starts. Plaintiff list as in U53 (tracker; court order caption agrees).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F8 / U37 / U46: CBO, work provision alone, enacted law: $325.6B in lower federal Medicaid outlays, FY2025-2034 (KFF rounds it to $326B). SECONDARY-VERIFIED: CRS citing CBO's 2025-07-21 estimate of PL 119-21. cbo.gov blocked the fetch.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U46 / F8 / G14: \"CBO says the savings come mostly from coverage losses, not added employment.\" CORRECTED: CRS R48633 does not say this. The words are KFF's own characterization. The \"no employment effect\" point is KFF relaying an EARLIER CBO analysis (of an earlier proposal), not the score of the enacted law. Right wording: \"KFF, reading CBO's numbers, says the savings come largely from people losing coverage; CBO's earlier work found a work requirement would not meaningfully raise employment among enrollees.\" Error source: engine finding (mis-attributed to CRS/CBO).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U37 / F8: CBO, enacted law: the work provision adds 5.3M uninsured in 2034 BEFORE interactions. More frequent redeterminations add 0.7M more. SECONDARY-VERIFIED (Georgetown CCF; the CBO uninsured table itself returned 403).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U86 / F8: the 18.5M-subject, 5.2M-losing-Medicaid and 4.8M-uninsured counts are from the HOUSE-PASSED bill. CBO has published no enacted-law count of people losing Medicaid. KFF also relays CBO's $8.65B revenue loss from indirect effects. SECONDARY-VERIFIED (KFF).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G14 context: the WHOLE law's health provisions add 10.0M uninsured in FY2034; the Medicaid provisions add 7.5M. SECONDARY-VERIFIED (CRS citing CBO 2025-08-11). Do not put 10M next to the work provision alone.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U36 / U45 / G11 / G14: the other CBO-attributed figures ($375B; $388B and 6M, the latter in a syndicated Scripps item) remain UNRESOLVED. Scope and vintage are unknown; they may include interactions or other provisions. CRS sources its $325.6B to CBO's July 2025 estimate, and that is the figure to use. Do not air $375B or $388B.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F7: CMS's RIA puts disenrollment at about 15% of the whole adult group. A 12% noncompliance rate applies only to the 74% who are subject and not excluded. The 7% net procedural rate applies to compliant and excepted people.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F7: the 7% is CMS's assumption for people who ARE working, in school or excepted but lose coverage over paperwork. APPROXIMATE: CMS's own list of reasons includes not only mail and form trouble but also improper or fraudulent enrollees and people who have other coverage. The figure is a scenario-weighted rate taken from post-unwinding renewal data and halved to strip out baseline attrition. Both sides may cite it, but it is not a pure count of eligible people wrongly cut.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F7: enrollment falls 2.3M in FY2027 (three quarters of a year) and 3.1-3.3M a year after that. Federal spending falls $350.3B over 10 years and state spending $41.6B. These are CMS's figures, not CBO's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F7: the RIA does not model behaviour change (more work or school) or people moving to other coverage, and says both could partly offset the losses.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F7: paperwork burden. 56% of about 20M applicable people verified ex parte, a rate taken from 2025 renewal data. The other 44% (8.8M) must send something. 35.2M hours a year at renewal, plus 7.5M hours a year for applications, at $12.92 an hour. CORRECTION to the engine note: about $455M a year is CMS's OWN figure ($454,784,000), not our arithmetic.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U71 / F7 / G11: systems cost is $15M per state x 44 states = $660M one-time, plus 10% a year to maintain. Total $1.52B for 2026-2036: federal $1.289B, states $231M. It rests on 21 state APDs averaging $12.2M, and half the states had reported no cost. The federal share is 90% for building and 75% for upkeep. This covers systems ONLY: no caseworkers, call centres or hearings.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U33 / F7: good-faith delays. CMS assumes about 10 states will ask and about 2 will be approved. This is a paperwork-burden planning assumption.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U32: good-faith exemptions. The first grant runs no longer than 6 months. Extensions are possible, but nothing can run past 2028-12-31. That date is a ceiling, not a promise.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F1 / U105: the SNAP exclusion. CORRECTED wording: the exclusion goes to an INDIVIDUAL who lives in a household getting SNAP AND who is personally subject to a SNAP work requirement. It does not go to members of a SNAP household \"that is not exempt\". Being on SNAP alone is not enough. Error source: engine finding's paraphrase.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F2: the caregiver exclusion's \"dependent child\" means a child 13 or under.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F1: the veterans exclusion requires a TOTAL (100%) VA rating, permanent or temporary. A temporary total rating is reverified every 12 months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U69: the optional high-unemployment hardship applies where unemployment is at least the lesser of 8% or 1.5 times the national rate. The rest of 435.555 was not re-read; the finder read it from the same primary.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F4 / U83 (rule part): data first, then ask. A sworn statement or other information is acceptable for frailty through 2027-12-31. From 2028-01-01 documentation is generally required when no reliable data exists. MACPAC's Arkansas soft-launch item in U83 was not checked (pass E's lane).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U70 / F5 open question: \"verification must be at least every 12 months\" is CORRECTED. The 12-month floor is for REVERIFYING MEDICAL FRAILTY, not for checking work. Work compliance is checked at each regular renewal: every 6 months for expansion adults, every 12 months for some section 1115 enrollees. States may check more often. Error source: engine finding conflated the two clocks.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F5 / U15 / G14: the STATUTE (PL 119-21 sec. 71119, now SSA 1902(xx)) is read directly. An applicant shows 1 to 3 consecutive months before applying, the state choosing the number. An enrollee shows 1 or more months, as the state specifies.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U75 / F5: income averaging over 6 months is in the statute ONLY for seasonal workers (26 U.S.C. 45R(d)(5)(B)). Everyone else passes a month on 80 hours or on minimum wage x 80 ($580 at $7.25, per the IFR).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F6: due process. A notice of noncompliance comes with 30 calendar days to cure, and the notice is deemed received 5 days after its date. The reapplication and reconsideration route (U103/U104) was not re-read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U101 / F6: the marketplace bar. Someone otherwise eligible for Medicaid but cut off for noncompliance cannot get ACA premium tax credits. The statute does this by deeming them eligible for Medicaid-type coverage, IRC 36B(c)(2)(B). Exchange-coordination guidance is still to come. (U101's quoted phrase is accurate but runs across a line break in the source.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F21: the data-matching notice. It re-establishes a match in place since 2013, with comments due 2026-11-30. The odd \"18 months, about Oct 2025 to Mar 2027\" term is printed that way in the notice itself. APPROXIMATE: it names no payroll, SNAP or TANF source, but it DOES list VA disability ratings, educational enrollment data and SSA benefit and \"current income sources\". So it carries exemption- and school-relevant data, not only tax income. Still not an hours database.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U11: Gresham v. Azar (D.C. Cir., 2020-02-14) affirmed the ruling against Arkansas's approval as arbitrary and capricious, citing more than 18,000 people (about 25% of those subject) losing coverage in five months. APPROXIMATE: the court called coverage \"a principal objective\" of Medicaid, not \"the principal objective\". Kentucky moved to dismiss its appeal as moot after ending its program.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U12: the Supreme Court granted review (2020-12-04) but never decided the merits. On 2022-04-18 it granted the government's motion to vacate, under Munsingwear, sending Arkansas (19-5094/5096) back for dismissal as moot and New Hampshire (19-5293/5295) back for remand to the Secretary. Say \"vacated as moot\", not \"wiped off the books\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U35: the Tribal Technical Advisory Group letter (2026-07-08) backs no reverification of AI/AN status. It asks CMS to make self-attestation the standard for the tribal exclusion and wants Tribal Consultation and Urban Confer before CMS finalizes changes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F3 (as written in June): the IFR's added test, quoted by the court as the rule text, applied on top of membership in one of the five groups. VACATED 2026-10-29 (A1). From now on it may only be described in the past tense.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a38"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F9/U1/U47: 18,164 people lost Arkansas Works coverage in 2018 for not meeting the work/reporting rule. Checked at the STATE's own layer (DHS December 2018 report, p.8 closures table): 4,353 + 4,109 + 3,815 + 4,655 + 1,232 = 18,164, across the five monthly closure rounds Aug-Dec 2018. The same report puts every closure in context: those five months closed 84,889 cases for all reasons (\"churn\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F9/U47: 11% (1,910) of the 18,164 regained coverage in 2019 (1,889 via Arkansas Works, 21 other). Correct as KFF's reading of the state's February 2019 data. It is an early snapshot: people closed in 2018 were locked out until January, so the 11% covers about two months of 2019, and the count kept moving (see B3).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"(new, G16) The state's own re-enrollment count at the start of January 2019 was 966. With the HHS Secretary's 1,452 (B17) and KFF's 1,910 (B2), the re-enrollment count is a moving number. Never quote it without its date.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F9: \"about 25% of those subject\", \"in five months\". This is the D.C. Circuit's own wording (Gresham v. Azar, Feb 14 2020), citing the DHS December 2018 report. The denominator is the 2018 subject population (60,000-73,000 a month), NOT the 116,229 of February 2019 that U1 sets beside 18,164.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F9/U49: \"about 27% of those REQUIRED TO REPORT (more than 16,000 of 60,012 in August 2018) did not report 80 hours\". WRONG BASE. 60,012 is everyone subject to the requirement in August 2018, including the roughly two-thirds the state excused from reporting by data match. Right wording: \"in August 2018, 16,357 of the 60,012 people subject to the rule (27%) did not meet it\". Among those who actually had to report, non-reporting was far higher (KFF, February 2019: 88% did not report 80 hours). Source of the error: the engine's rewording of KFF's sentence.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U49: data matching excused about two-thirds from reporting. KFF breakdown of that group: 46% already working 80+ hours, 18% SNAP E&T, 13% dependent child, 15% medically frail. (U49's last sentence, Arkansas's 2026 waiver admission, was NOT checked here.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U2/U31/F9: Health Affairs 2020, triple difference: −13.2 points Medicaid/Marketplace and +7.1 points uninsured for ages 30-49 in 2018; by 2019 (court-halted) no significant difference. The HA 2020 text gives no CIs (they are in a figure); the same 2018 point estimates with their CIs are printed in NEJM 2019 (B9).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U16/U31/U82: unadjusted rates, Arkansans 30-49. Coverage 70.5% → 63.7% → 66.1% (2016/18/19); uninsured 10.5% → 14.6% → 12.5%. Among those who disenrolled for ANY reason in the past year (N=117): 49.8% serious medical-debt problems, 55.9% delayed care, 63.8% delayed medications; median debt $1,001 vs $0. These are descriptive, not causal (the authors say so; see B12).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U3/G16, LOAD-BEARING: NEJM 2019 read at source (Table 2, Table 3). Coverage −13.2 (95% CI −23.3 to −3.2, P=0.01); uninsured +7.1 (95% CI 0.5 to 13.6, P=0.04); uninsured 10.5% → 14.5% (NEJM's 2018 wave; HA 2020 reports 14.6% for the same year with the later sample); EMPLOYMENT triple difference −1.1 points (95% CI −8.7 to 6.5, P=0.78). NEJM figures are the first 6 months only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U3: NEJM: 32.9% of 30-49 enrollees had heard nothing about the rule; \"nearly 97%\" of Arkansans 30-49 already met the requirement or should have been exempt (survey-based; \"more than 95%\" in the discussion). The authors say the 18,164 is consistent with their estimate (a 12-point enrollment drop on a frame of about 140,000).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U3: Health Affairs: 70.8% of all Arkansans unsure whether the rule was in effect (late 2019); 34.9% of 30-49s with recent coverage had heard nothing. Only 11.3% preferred the computer website, the only reporting route for most of 2018.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U25/U31/U4: the authors' own limitations: 15% response rate, recall and social-desirability bias, no linked administrative enrollment data, cannot attribute which losses the policy caused, possible unobserved time-varying confounders, one state. The employment comparison is 2018 (in force) against 2019 (blocked).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F10/U82/U108: \"where the 16,000+ went is UNMEASURED\". OVERSTATED. Health Affairs 2020 did measure where survey respondents ended up after disenrolling: of 117 Arkansans aged 30-49 who left coverage for ANY reason, 55.6% had re-enrolled, 8.2% had employer coverage, 10.1% other insurance and 26.1% were uninsured. Right wording: \"no source tracks the 18,164 themselves; a small survey of people who lost coverage for any reason found about a quarter uninsured and under one in ten on employer coverage\". The non-significant rise in employer coverage (U82) is confirmed. Source of the error: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U4/U108: the state's objection. The dossier sources the \"not a 'meaningful or thorough' evaluation\" quote to the Health Affairs paper (PMC7497731). It is not there. It was a DHS spokesperson's June 2019 statement on the NEJM study, carried by news reports; the saved text could not confirm it verbatim. What I could confirm, in KFF Health News, is the state's design objection: under a year, no follow-up on why people did not reapply, a request to continue with a longitudinal evaluation. Use a role (\"the state's Medicaid spokesperson\"), not the name. Source of the error: engine finding (wrong source attached).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G16, LOAD-BEARING: the strongest published critique, read at source: FGA, July 29 2019 (advocacy). Its points: (a) \"sloppy methodology\"; (b) a survey, not administrative data, at a 15% response rate, with recall bias; (c) the sample included people never subject (parents, disabled, above poverty); (d) the survey came too early; (e) only 13% of case closures were for non-compliance; (f) the margin of error; (g) employer coverage rose in Arkansas; (h) state data show 17,700 left for higher income and 9,200 found jobs. Assessment: (b) and (d) are fair and the authors concede them. (c) is real, but it dilutes estimates toward zero: it makes the coverage drop conservative and the employment test weaker. (f) compares the overlap of two separate level estimates; the paper's own test of the change (+7.1, CI 0.5 to 13.6) excludes zero, though its lower end is near zero. (e) does not rebut a design that compares against other ages and states. (h) is unsourced in the saved text and was not verified. The dossier's \"more than a million outreach communications\" and \"92% smartphone ownership\" are NOT in this FGA paper (grep: no hits), so their source is unknown.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U87/G16: the independent ACS re-analysis (Urban Institute and Loyola authors; RWJF-funded, as was Sommers). Read at ABSTRACT level on RWJF's page; the journal article itself was not opened. Uninsurance +4.4 points (randomization-inference p = 0.04), +7.4 points below 100% FPL; employment −0.7 points (p = 0.62); no effect in unaffected groups. Unlike Sommers, it pools 2018-2019 against 2016-2017. The Hilltop paper in U87 was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F10/U66: the HHS Secretary's two statements (House: no data on why people left; Senate Finance two days later: low re-enrollment \"a fairly strong indication\" they got jobs), and the New Hire match of 1,981 of 18,164 with its caveats. Secondary-verified through CBPP (advocacy, March 2019); the hearing transcripts and the state's New Hire release were not opened. KFF Health News separately reports the Senate figure as \"only 1,452 of the 18,000\" reapplying (see B3: the count moved).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U5/F12, LOAD-BEARING: GAO-25-108160 read at source. FY2021 through Q2 FY2025 (ended Mar 31 2025): administration $54,179,661, medical assistance $26,100,696, total $80,280,357, administration share 67.5%. Federal share of administration $47,438,365 (87.6% in the table; \"about 88 percent\" in the text).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U72/F12: what GAO says is MISSING from the $54.2M: (1) further FY2025 spending with no estimate, including $6.0M approved for FY2025 system work that is reported under a $430.0M enterprise-wide IT project; (2) $20M of other federal grants (ARPA) used for marketing campaigns; (3) expected staff training, stakeholder coordination and evaluation costs. Georgia officials estimated spending was 20-30% higher because of the pause. Eligibility-system spending: $50.8M ($45.1M contractors, $5.8M in-house). F12's \"a share of a $430M enterprise IT project\" is loose: GAO identifies only the $6.0M as Pathways-related inside that project. Right wording: \"$6 million approved for 2025 system work, booked under a $430 million statewide IT project\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"(new, the supporters' best rebuttal) GAO Table 2: the administration share falls year by year: 100%, 100%, 96.5%, 58.8%, then 2.3% in the first half of FY2025. GAO attributes the overall share to up-front set-up, the delayed start and duplication from the delay. The 67.5% is a cumulative start-up figure, not a running cost ratio.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U6/F12: enrollment. The December 2019 application projected over 25,000 full-year-equivalent enrollees in year 1; the actual figure was under 3,500 in the first 15 months; 7,463 enrolled as of May 2025; the extension request projects just over 18,000 in year 1 and over 30,000 in year 5. CMS blamed the requirement to show compliance in the month before applying. Monthly-reporting suspensions were paused, BUT (new) people can still be disenrolled at annual renewal. Also new: 15,000 applicants were instead approved for traditional Medicaid (July 2023 to September 2024).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U7/F12: Georgia DCH public notice dated August 13 2026. About 20,000 covered (internal data); nearly 120,000 by June 30 2028; asks 90% instead of 66.86% FMAP; fiscal impact $1,433,200,000 for Jan 1 2027 to Jun 30 2028; says it cannot continue Pathways as is because P.L. 119-21's conditions are unwaivable. F12's reason (\"the federal exclusions its waiver lacked now apply\") is loose: the notice says the new law forces it to cover about 100,000 more people. $1.433B is the TOTAL cost (state $143.3M + federal $1.29B in the notice's table), not the size of the extra federal ask. Whether CMS has decided was not checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U26/F12: BMJ (Household Pulse, 17,451 adults ≤100% FPL). U26 says Medicaid coverage in Georgia \"fell from 35.5% to 32.4%\". The paper says it \"did not change\": the difference is not significant (−3.0 points, CI −7.5 to 1.4; difference-in-differences against neighbours −3.0, CI −7.6 to 1.6). Employment against neighbours −1.6 (CI −8.7 to 5.4); against South Dakota −0.1 (CI −9.8 to 9.6). The one significant result: coverage −11.7 points against South Dakota (CI −19.5 to −3.9), where coverage rose 8.0. The authors' own limitations: self-report and a low response rate. Source of the error: engine wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U94: GBPI (advocacy-leaning): about $110M through June 2025; benefits about 31%; eligibility IT about $52M (47%); 13,369 ever enrolled; 8,077 active (4,231 at year 1); year 2 awareness campaign about $21M ARPA against about $25M benefits; about $13,597 per active enrollee. The per-enrollee figure divides cumulative cost, including one-time IT, by those enrolled on one date. It is not an annual cost per person.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U8/F11: Kentucky HEALTH. The 95,000 is Kentucky's own estimate of people who would leave the rolls over 5 years, not a count. The court vacated the re-approval on March 27 2019; the re-approval would have taken effect April 1 2019. The December 16 2019 end of the project was NOT checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U9/F11: New Hampshire. As of July 8 2019, about 17,000 of about 25,000 non-exempt people had reported nothing; enforcement was delayed to Sept 30 and losses to Dec 1; approval vacated July 29 2019; \"particularly startling\" given Arkansas.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U10/F11: Michigan implemented January 1 2020, stopped by court order March 4 2020; the state's mandated Section 1509 report gives no non-compliance count (the full report is these two paragraphs). The minute-order wording quoted in U10 was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F11: the Supreme Court (No. 20-37, April 18 2022) vacated the D.C. Circuit judgments in the Arkansas and New Hampshire appeals (19-5094, 19-5096) with instructions to dismiss as moot (Munsingwear). Gresham is not binding precedent.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U89 (abstract-worded claim): \"by April 2019, when a federal judge halted the policy, more than 18,000 had lost coverage\". The Health Affairs abstract does say \"By April 2019\". But the halting ruling (Gresham, D.D.C.) was filed March 27 2019, and all 18,164 losses were 2018 closures. Right wording: \"a federal judge halted it in March 2019, after about 18,000 people had lost coverage in 2018\". Source of the error: the source's own abstract wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U96: Urban \"most losses came from reporting problems; about 89% already work, seek work, study or are exempt\". The Urban report itself could not be opened (the CHA copy is behind a login; the urban.org URL returned 404). The RWJF summary confirms only the general conclusion, which is a PROJECTION about a future federal rule, not a measured breakdown of the Arkansas and New Hampshire losses. The 89% is unverified.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"FGA's counter-numbers (B15h): \"17,700 left for increased income, 9,200 found new employment\". The state's own closures table shows \"Household Increased Income\" as 10-15% of ALL monthly closures, which is ordinary churn that the table does not attribute to the requirement. The 9,200 job figure has no source in the saved state report. Neither number can be aired as an effect of the requirement.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U27/F18: ASPE's June 2026 brief says work requirements \"increased employment by 4.2 percentage points in the first five years\", drawn from three early-1990s NEWWS randomized trials. Checked at the brief itself. The wording is right and the date (Jun 1, 2026) is right. The title is \"…Incentivize Employment and Are Estimated to Reduce Poverty\", not the dossier URL's \"should-incentivize\". What the 4.2 is: requirements bundled with services, measured against AFDC-era cash with no mandate, for single parents on cash welfare. It is not a Medicaid result. The NEWWS attribution is CORRECTED in C3.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U27 (method of the 4.2): the brief says the figure comes from a 2025 Mathematica systematic review done for ASPE. Its technical appendix shows the 4.2 is NOT a raw difference from one trial. It is an average of standardized effect sizes across the studies in the \"work requirements\" bin, converted back into 2018 percentage points against a CPS low-earner distribution. \"Long term\" means anything from 18 months to 5 years. Script: it is \"an average across a few 1990s trials\", never \"a study found 4.2\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U27/F18/G17 (which trials): ASPE's own study table (132 studies) tags only seven rows with the strategy \"Work requirements\". Five are welfare-waiver experiments: Connecticut Jobs First (1996), Minnesota MFIP (1994, two rows) and Vermont WRP (1994, two rows). The other two are the SNAP studies, Gray et al. (Virginia) and Han (national), and both are short-term only. None of the NEWWS sites (Atlanta, Grand Rapids, Riverside, Columbus, Detroit, Oklahoma City, Portland) carries the \"Work requirements\" tag. They are tagged work-first, supports or case management. So the brief's text (\"three … NEWWS trials\") conflicts with its own appendix. The three long-term work-requirement RCTs appear to be Jobs First, MFIP and WRP. That last step is my inference from the table, because the appendix does not print the per-strategy calculation. All three are mid-1990s AFDC experiments, and each bundled a mandate with earnings incentives or supports. Source of the error: the source's own wording (ASPE), which the engine relayed.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F18/U27/G8 (the poverty simulation): 1.6-2.9M fewer in poverty is a static TRIM3 microsimulation. Scenario 1 (2.9M) assumes every eligible adult enrolls and immediately finds enough work. Scenario 2 (1.6M) assumes 85% take-up and 80% finding work. ASPE says itself that the model leaves out administrative-burden losses (the coverage-loss channel) and that the results are illustrative.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F18/U27 (Alabama TANF, Charlotte housing): ASPE's wording is confirmed. The Alabama figure links to cbo.gov publication 58867 and the Charlotte figure to a 2016 Housing Policy Debate article (doi 10.1080/10511482.2015.1137967). Both returned 403 and were NOT opened, so the underlying designs are unverified. As ASPE describes Charlotte, it is a one-site before/after with no comparison group, timed to a strong job market. Air both only as \"ASPE cites…\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U13/U22/U24/F17: Gray, Leive, Prager, Pukelis and Zaki use a Virginia regression discontinuity at age 50 and linked SNAP and earnings records. Confirmed: exits up 23 points (64%) at 18 months, participation down 53%, homeless adults screened out, no employment effect, and gains above 3.5 points ruled out. Read at the NBER paper (full text).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U22/F17 (venue): published as American Economic Journal: Economic Policy 15(1): 306-341 (2023). F17's \"AEJ: Policy\" should read \"AEJ: Economic Policy\". The published abstract keeps the same 23-point / 64% / 53% headline and the same null on employment.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U38/F17: Cai, Kim and Leung (arXiv v2, 18 Sep 2026, NOT peer-reviewed; five states; triple differences). Confirmed: participation down 4.4 points (7%), employment -0.4 points (not significant, 95% interval -1 to +0.2), earnings -$15 a quarter (4 states). Two nuances the dossier left out. Their alternative specifications show positive employment effects, which the authors discount because of pre-trends. They also find requirements do NOT disproportionately screen out the non-employed: the screening is by income.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F17 (\"no rigorous study of a Medicaid or SNAP work requirement finds an employment gain\"): too strong. Two peer-reviewed SNAP studies using survey data find gains. Harris (2021, Economic Inquiry) finds about +1 point. Cuffey, Beatty and Mykerezi (AJAE) find a large effect for low-income, low-education adults (C10). Right wording: \"the studies using state administrative records find no employment gain; two survey-based studies find a small gain or a gain for one disadvantaged subgroup.\" Source of the error: the engine's finding (it never read Harris or Cuffey).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G17 (the supporters' strongest peer-reviewed SNAP result): Cuffey, Beatty and Mykerezi, \"Work Effort and Work Requirements for Food Assistance among U.S. Adults\", American Journal of Agricultural Economics (online 2021). The design is a CPS regression discontinuity at age 50. For low-income, low-education adults, aging out of the requirement lowers employment, which implies the requirement raises work for that group. The authors doubt the aggregate effect is large. Abstract-only read. Cai's appendix puts the estimate at +13 points on a 35% base.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G17 (Han, a study ASPE itself puts in the work-requirements bin): suspending the SNAP requirement did not discourage employment, so the requirement did not raise it. Abstract-only (SSRN 2018 version; published in Labour Economics 2022 per ASPE's table).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U73/F17: Census working paper CES-24-54 (Keene and Carpenter, Sept 2024; restricted SNAP microdata from nine states). Abstract-only read: no discontinuity in employment at the ABAWD age cutoff. Under the general work requirement, unemployed users are more likely to leave the labour force than keep searching.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U88/U91/F17: Maryland (Hall, Univ. of Maryland School of Social Work, Aug 2022; a university report, not peer-reviewed). Confirmed: 44-46% employed either way, and earnings up 61% vs 58% (these are MEDIAN quarterly earnings). Participation fell 65 vs 54 points. Hall's own literature summary credits Harris with \"at best\" about +1 point.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U14/F18: the MDRC 2001 synthesis (Bloom and Michalopoulos). The claim that each of the 20 mandatory-services programs raised earnings is overstated. MDRC says programs of all types were effective \"to some extent\", that each JOB-SEARCH-FIRST program raised earnings by at least $400 a year, and that MOST education-first programs raised them. The best programs ran more than 10 points above control. Families were usually no better off financially. Source of the error: the engine's paraphrase.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U14 (time limits): confirmed. Time limits did not markedly change employment impacts. In two programs with earnings supplements plus time limits, the income gains disappeared at the limit and income fell for a small group.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U80/F18 (NEWWS durability): read in MDRC's copy of the ASPE/ACF five-year report (Hamilton et al., Dec 2001), because webharvest blocked automated reads. Confirmed: only 3 of 11 programs raised ever-employment by more than 3 points (Riverside LFA 8.4, Riverside HCD 5.8, Portland 4.1). Controls found jobs at high rates anyway, and the programs did not raise combined income. The Portland \"faded after years 8-9\" part was NOT opened (MDRC page unreachable) and stays unverified.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U21/F18: Kansas DCF's own testimony (House Oversight subcommittees, June 9, 2016). Confirmed: ABAWD caseload 27,224 to 7,511, and the work rate \"nearly tripled\" (Jul-Sep 2013 vs Feb-Apr 2014). Nearly half of leavers were employed the next quarter. Trap: the \"tripled\" rate is among ABAWDs still ON SNAP after non-workers were removed, which is a change in who is left, not proof that anyone started working. The agency says FGA analysed the data. There is no comparison group.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U64/F18: the FGA appendix attached to the DCF testimony. Confirmed as FGA's claim: about 13,000 left on 31 Dec 2013, nearly 60% employed within 12 months, incomes +127% a year, more than offsetting lost food stamps. These are before/after figures on leavers with no counterfactual. Maryland's comparison group (C13) saw almost the same earnings growth.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U45/F18: Maine. CORRECTED. It was a \"cumulative 114 percent increase in WAGES\" for \"a subset\" of the roughly 6,900 removed people, Q4 2014 to Q4 2015, counting only those with reported Maine wages. It was not \"individual incomes … rose 114 percent within a year\", and that quoted headline does not appear in the article. The only benchmark was statewide wage growth. The co-author (state economist) called it preliminary and made no causal claim. Secondary-verified: the state report itself was not opened; I read the Bangor Daily News, 11 May 2016. Source of the error: secondary relay.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U18/F22: KFF Feb 2025. Confirmed: fielded Feb 18-25, 2025, n=1,322, ±3. 62% support (R 82, I 60, D 47-53). Wording: \"nearly all adults to be working or looking for work\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U19/F22: Feb 2025 message tests. Confirmed: 62 to 32 (paperwork), 62 to 40 (no employment effect plus admin cost), 62 to 77 (the \"reserved for\" argument). The pro argument in Feb was \"ensure Medicaid is reserved for\" (June changed it to \"save money, helping fund\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U20/F22: KFF June 2025 topline. Confirmed: June 4-8, 2025, n=1,321, ±3. 68% support the version WITH caregiver and disability exceptions and school or community service. 52% of supporters still support after the paperwork message (35% of all adults). 59% still support after the no-employment-effect message (40% of all adults). The June question wording differs from February's, so 62 to 68 is NOT a trend.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U20/F22 (June party splits): confirmed at KFF's June report: R 88, MAGA 93, D 51.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U76/F22: CORRECTED (minor). 36% of opponents flipped (n=445), not 37%. That is 12% of all adults; KFF's report gives total support as 79% (\"an 11 point increase\"), while the topline combo sums to 68+12. KFF flags the argument as not factually accurate. The same argument flipped 41% of opponents in February. Source of the error: engine arithmetic.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U76 (KFF's own flag): confirmed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U77/F22 (awareness gap, June 2025): confirmed. 56% say most are unemployed (it was 62% in Feb, so the gap narrowed). More than 3 in 4 Republicans are unaware; D 57% and I 48% are aware.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U77/F22 (working enrollees): confirmed. 53% of working Medicaid enrollees under 65 support it, dropping to a quarter after the paperwork trade-off.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F22/U98/G17 (Paragon's \"more than 80 percent\"): CORRECTED, wording now seen. It is the \"Paragon Health Policy Survey 2025\" by Public Opinion Strategies: 1,000 REGISTERED VOTERS, Feb 25-Mar 2, 2025, ±3.53. Results: 81% support \"requiring able-bodied adult Medicaid recipients to work\" and 84% support \"allowing states\" to do so. The prompt says nothing about exemptions, about who already works, or about paperwork. KFF fielded almost the same week and found 62% with different wording (all adults, \"nearly all adults\"). The gap is a wording and sample difference, not a contradiction. Dossier said the wording was \"not seen\"; it is now.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U65/U98/F23: Paragon's IFC statement. Wording confirmed. CORRECTION: Paragon says ASPE estimates \"two to three million\" lifted out of poverty, but ASPE's range is 1.6 to 2.9 million (C4), so Paragon rounds the low end up. The \"seven times greater\" federal match has no basis given on the page.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U63/F23: Paragon \"Correcting the Record\" (July 22, 2025). Confirmed: the fairness/reciprocity argument, and \"a large and growing number … particularly men\" choosing not to work, with no count given. The only number is CBO's \"nearly five million\" who won't meet the requirement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U17/F23: the IFC preamble. Extended to the FULL document (871K characters, not a quarter): zero hits for \"reciproc\" or \"taxpayer\". Its stated rationale is self-sufficiency, alignment with SNAP/TANF, prioritising the most vulnerable, and \"a short-term hand up\". Its evidence claim is that work is ASSOCIATED with health. Confirmed: 82.4M enrolled, about 20M in the adult group, about $200B of nearly $1T.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U62/G17: AEI (Corinth), the best pro-side population analysis found. 2023 SIPP, December 2022: 44% of non-disabled, childless Medicaid adults worked 80+ hours vs 72% of non-recipients; 51% vs 84% at ages 25-54; 19.6M and 8.8M. The author himself says the gap with \"most work\" findings is single-month vs any-time-in-the-year. \"Non-disabled\" means no SSI or disability income, which counts some people who could claim the frailty exemption. The claim that the administration cited this figure was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U61: FGA \"X factor\". The page says 60% (not a 59-62% range) \"reported no earned income\": earned income REPORTED to Medicaid agencies, from 23 responding states holding about 57% of enrollees, extrapolated to 24.6M. The data are not public.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G8 (the shared behavioural premise): the Oregon Health Insurance Experiment (Baicker, Finkelstein, Song, Taubman; AER P&P 2014; a lottery RCT) found Medicaid had no significant effect on employment either way. That undercuts both \"Medicaid discourages work\" and \"coverage enables work\" as large effects. Abstract-only (NBER page).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G6 (service capacity / design): only partly answerable from my primaries. ASPE's own evidence is \"requirements PLUS supports\", yet its recommendation is only that states \"can consider\" pairing with job training. No workforce-board, Department of Labor or state Medicaid-director capacity documents were fetched. UNRESOLVED for capacity.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U80 (the Portland fade after years 8-9): not opened. MDRC's Portland long-term page refused the connection and webharvest blocked automated reads. This page shows only the five-year Portland impact (+4.1). Do not air \"faded after year 8/9\" until it is read.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U57/F15: KFF SIPP shares. POPULATION: Medicaid adults 19-64 in states that had expanded by Dec 2021, with no disability income, not on Medicare, not living with own children under 18, not pregnant, complete hours records. MEASURE: one month (June 2022), 80+ hours worked. 53% worked 80+ hrs, 9% in school, 15% illness/disability, 2% caregiving, 21% retired/couldn't find work/other. Chart values: 53.4 / 8.7 / 14.6 / 2.2 / 21. Note: the 21% bucket includes \"not interested\" (2-3% by age) and \"Other\" (no reason given), so it is not all \"could but doesn't\". Published Jun 25, 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U58: \"nearly eight in ten\" met the test or had an exemption-type reason. Same population as D1 (53.4 + 8.7 + 14.6 + 2.2 = 78.9%). The exclusion \"parents of dependent children under 18\" is WIDER than the rule's (a child 13 or under, D19), so parents of 14-17-year-olds, who ARE subject under the rule, are missing from KFF's denominator. \"Exemption-type\" is KFF's judgement, not a rule test (no frailty screen applied).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U40/F16: KFF six-month follow-up. The dossier says \"of those who met 80 hours in June, ONLY 44% met it in all six following months\". WRONG READING. KFF's chart sums to 100% of ALL subject adults (44 + 4 + 5 + 47 \"did not work 80+ in June\"). So 44 of the 53 points (about 83%) of June's 80-hour workers kept it every month July-Dec, and about 9 points (about 17% of June workers, 9% of all subject adults) dipped in at least one month. KFF's own headline is \"about 10% of Medicaid adults\". Right wording: \"most who worked 80 hours in June kept it every month for the next six; about one in six dipped at least once\". Error source: the engine, from KFF's ambiguous sentence; the chart data settles it.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U40/F16 (text side of D3): KFF's prose says most kept the hours, and that volatility matters mainly if states check monthly or look back over several months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U95: KFF CPS. POPULATION: ALL Medicaid adults 19-64 in ALL states with no SSI/SSDI and not on Medicare. This INCLUDES PARENTS (who work more: 72% vs 58% for adults without a child at home). MEASURE: worked at any point in 2023, full or part time, regardless of hours. 64% working; 92% working or caregiving/ill/disabled/school; 8% retired, couldn't find work or other. Not comparable with D1 (one-month 80-hour test, expansion only, no parents). CMS used THIS data for its noncompliance rate (D19).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U59/F15: Urban 31% / 21% / 2%. Answers G10's \"no stated survey year\". POPULATION: SIPP 2017-19 (pre-pandemic, chosen because continuous enrollment distorted 2020+), n = 4,422 Medicaid adults 19-64 in expansion states, no resident children under 18, not disability-eligible. MEASURE: the whole year. 31% = did not work or attend school in ANY month while enrolled. 46% met the test (80 hrs, school, or $580 earnings) in EVERY month; 23% worked or studied but missed at least one month. \"2%\" = jobless, not in school all year, citing lack of interest. The RWJF landing page dates it 11 June 2025; the PDF says June 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U61/F15: AEI (Kevin Corinth, AEIdeas blog post, Feb 28, 2025). POPULATION: Medicaid recipients in ALL states (not expansion-only), no SSI/disability income, not living with own children under 18. MEASURE: one month (Dec 2022), 80+ hours. Students are NOT set aside, so they count as not meeting it, whereas KFF lists them separately as compliant. 44% vs 72% for non-Medicaid adults; ages 25-54: 51% vs 84%; 19.6 million recipients 18-64. The piece explicitly rebuts annual-work measures like D5. The dossier's \"this is the figure cited in the administration's public case\" was NOT checked here.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F15 \"KFF and AEI largely agree on the raw count (about half work 80 hours in a month)\". Approximately right but not the same number or population: KFF 53% (June 2022, expansion states only) vs AEI 44% (Dec 2022, all states, students counted as not meeting it). Safe on-air form: \"in any one month of 2022, roughly half (between about 45% and 55%, depending on who's counted) worked 80 hours\". ⚠ Do not pair AEI's 44% with KFF's 44% (D3): they measure different things.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U94/F15 FGA. Dossier \"59-62% had no earned income\". CORRECTED: the cited FGA page (Jonathan Bain, July 19, 2024) says 60 percent; no 59-62 range appears on it. POPULATION: \"able-bodied adults\", which FGA's footnote defines as non-disabled, non-elderly adult enrollees. That INCLUDES PARENTS and non-expansion adults, so it is not the rule's subject group. MEASURE: zero earned income reported in state agency records (2019 and 2022), not monthly hours. 23 responding states (about 57% of enrollees); the state-level data is not published on the page; 24.6 million is FGA's national extrapolation. Error source: the engine (range not in the source).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U39/F16 Brookings/Hamilton (May 22, 2025). POPULATION: low-income SERVICE-SECTOR WORKERS aged 18-54 in general, not Medicaid enrollees. MEASURE: calendar 2022, any month under 80 hours. 64% fell short in at least one month; 34% of those AVERAGING 80+ did; 20% of the continuously employed averaging 80+ did; 42% of those without minor children (the Medicaid target) averaging 80+. Under the federal floor (1 qualifying month per review period) a single short month does not fail anyone.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U74/F16 JAMA Health Forum (Shafer, Callaghan, Anderson; published Aug 28, 2026). POPULATION: CPS ASEC 2023-25, adults 18-64 in 40 expansion states + DC who had Medicaid now or in the past year OR household income under 150% FPL (so it includes non-enrollees), after removing the rule's exemptions (parent of a child 13 or under, half-time school, work-limiting disability, AI/AN). MEASURE: WEEKLY hours. \"At risk\" = usual hours within ±5 of 20/week and/or last week's hours ±5 off usual. 19.8% of WORKERS (not of everyone) were \"at risk\" (13.6% near, 7.6% variable, 1.4% both); 66.6% in the labour force, 85.3% of them employed. \"At risk\" includes people usually working 21-25 hours who pass. Authors say the estimates may be conservative where states require several months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G10 (who is actually left subject under the rule's own definitions): CMS's RIA in the IFR answers how the official 12% / 7% / ~15% premise was built. 24% of the adult group are specifically excluded (2.5% AI/AN + 21.7% pregnant/postpartum, parent or caregiver of a child 13 or under or of a disabled person, inmates, medically frail); 3.45% get short-term hardship; 74% must show compliance. The 12% noncompliance rate is taken from KFF's CPS work-status data (D5): full-time workers always pass, 75-100% of part-timers pass by scenario, non-workers without a care or health reason always fail, so 75-81% meet it. The 7% net procedural rate is half of a 12% (semi-annual) or 20% (quarterly) gross rate anchored to post-unwinding renewal data. Result: about 15% of adult-group enrollment; 2.3M fewer in FY2027, 3.1-3.3M later. ★ The federal government's own coverage-loss arithmetic rests on KFF's survey. No published re-run applies the frailty test or the SNAP/TANF-compliance exclusion to survey data.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U41/F19 KFF unwinding survey (published Apr 12, 2024). POPULATION: adults who had Medicaid in early 2023, surveyed Feb 15-Mar 11, 2024 (n = 1,227, ±4). MEASURE: SELF-REPORTED disenrollment (19% said they were disenrolled). Of the self-reported disenrolled: 23% uninsured now, 47% re-enrolled, 28% other coverage. Fielded MID-unwinding, with about 1 in 3 enrollees not yet through renewal, so it is a snapshot, not an endpoint. Among ALL prior enrollees, 8% were uninsured at survey time (6% in expansion states, 17% in non-expansion states).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U42/F19: same survey, full report. 70% of the disenrolled became uninsured at the time (at least temporarily); 56% skipped or delayed care while trying to renew. The disenrolled subgroup is only n = 253 with a ±8-point margin, so 70%, 56% and 23% each carry ±8. The 70% → 23% gap supports \"often a gap, not a permanent loss\" only for this mid-unwinding snapshot.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G12/G18: the dossier treats Federal Reserve FEDS 2025-008 as an unread MEDICAL-DEBT paper. CORRECTED: it is about insurance coverage and household economic security (Household Pulse self-reports, state-month timing of unwinding; preliminary staff paper, Oct 2024). It finds Medicaid enrollment fell 6-12% but NO statistically significant national effect on being uninsured, on private insurance or on economic security. Caveat: survey undercount of Medicaid. This is the observed-data counterweight G18 says is missing (it points the other way from the simulations). It has no medical-debt measure.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U90/F19 California ED study (Khodakarami et al., Health Affairs Scholar 2026; full text read via PMC). OBSERVED data: 44.6M outpatient ED visits, 281 hospitals, 2021-2024. CORRECTED on two points. (1) The 0.37-pp Medicaid fall is a PER-QUARTER TREND change, not a one-time drop \"during unwinding\"; there was no immediate level shift. (2) F19's \"most at rural hospitals\" is wrong: rural hospitals had the LARGEST INCREASE in uninsured share (+0.29 pp/quarter, +46.7% relative to a small base), not most of the visits. Authors call the effects \"measurable, but modest\": after 6 quarters uninsured share +0.96 pp, about 171,000 visits, about $121M. California auto-enrolled disenrollees into Marketplace plans, so effects may be larger in other states. Error source: the engine relaying the abstract's compressed wording.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U43/F19 Commonwealth Fund brief (Sept 18, 2025; Dobson DaVanzo HFSM). SIMULATION, one year (2027, full implementation assumed), 2,958 acute-care hospitals. Uncompensated care +$7.0-8.0B (+29.5-33.6%); revenue -$10.9-12.4B; margins -11.7 to -13.3%. Hidden inputs a viewer should know: coverage loss is Urban's 5.5-6.3M (CBO: 5.2M, by 2034), and 92% of those losing Medicaid are ASSUMED to become uninsured (CBO's assumption). Compare the observed unwinding: 23% still uninsured at survey time (D13). DSH and local offsets are deliberately not modelled.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U44: same brief, safety-net and rural safety-net hospitals. 567 safety-net (deemed-DSH) hospitals: margins -25.9 to -29.6%, uncompensated care +38.2 to 43.5%. 316 rural safety-net hospitals: net operating income -24.6 to -28.0%. 11 states: safety-net uncompensated care +50% or more. A 2027 start may not leave time for automatic verification.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U60 (AEH, trade association; page dated June 4, 2026): $466B added \"uncompensated care\" 2025-2034 from the whole law plus expired ACA credits; 10M more uninsured in 2034 + 4.2M from the credits. CORRECTED detail: the page says essential hospitals are 6% (not \"5%\") of acute-care hospitals and bear 25%. The \"earlier estimate\" was not seen. ⚠ AEH's \"uncompensated care\" INCLUDES the Medicaid payment shortfall, so it is a different measure from Commonwealth's unpaid care for the uninsured; never set them side by side. Not work-requirement-specific.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U97 AHA (news item, June 16, 2025, on the HOUSE bill's \"key Medicaid provisions\"): $50.4B less federal Medicaid spending on rural hospitals over 10 years; 1.8M rural people losing Medicaid by 2034. The fact sheet itself was not opened; the news item does not isolate work requirements.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U106 (part 1): Lindrooth et al., Health Affairs 2018, abstract (2008-16 data). Expansion was associated with better hospital finances and lower closure risk, especially in rural markets. An association study; abstract only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U106 (part 2): the 2021 Health Affairs critical-access-hospital study is Chatterjee, Werner and Joynt Maddox (PMID 34871072), abstract. Difference-in-differences: no statistically significant post-expansion margin gain at critical access hospitals, and no staffing or quality gain.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G4/G18 offsetting money: the $50B Rural Health Transformation Program (CMS release via HHS, Dec 29, 2025; read through r.jina.ai because hhs.gov returned 403). $10B a year 2026-2030. FY2026 awards: Nebraska $218.5M, Georgia $218.9M, Montana $233.5M, Arkansas $208.8M. The stated uses are workforce, technology and care coordination, not uncompensated-care backfill, so \"it offsets the hospital losses\" is NOT shown. No state plan was read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G4 insurer view (Centene Q3 2026 call, Oct 27, 2026). SECONDARY: a third-party transcript (bullfincher.io); the company's own Q3 2026 earnings release does not discuss work requirements. Management says much of the 2026 membership drop (8-9% vs YE2025) is states tightening eligibility before the rule, \"a pull forward\" of the work-requirement acuity shift, and it expects disenrollments from the rule. Relevance: an at-stake insurer expects the members who stay to be SICKER on average (acuity rises), which implies those who leave are lower-cost on average. Transcript errors are possible (\"early 2020\" is presumably 2029).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F19 summary sentence \"after coverage is lost, the evidence is mostly self-reports and simulations\". APPROXIMATE, now with observed pieces on both sides. Observed: the California ED payer-mix shift (D16, modest, concentrated at small and rural hospitals) and the Fed's null national uninsurance effect (D15). Self-report: the KFF survey (D13-D14). Simulation: Commonwealth/AEH/AHA (D17-D20). Nothing observed from Nebraska, Georgia or Arkansas hospitals was found in this pass. The Commonwealth brief itself flags its uncertainty.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U28/F13: Nebraska renewals Jul–Aug 2026: 7,280 needed a work-rule determination; 533 denied; 490 (91.9%) for not responding, 43 for non-compliance. Now read at the state's own document. 533/7,280 = 7.3% lost coverage; 490/7,280 = 6.7% of renewals were non-response losses. The 92% is a share of the 533 losses.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U29/F13: the \"97%\" is 2,368 of the 2,449 who qualified through activities (96.7%), by working 80 hours OR having at least $580 in a qualifying month. It is not \"97% passed the income test\" and not 97% of renewals.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U29/U67: Georgetown CCF's wording turns the 97% into an income-only test. Wrong: the state's category is 80 hours OR $580. Right wording: \"about 97% of those who qualified through activities did it by working 80 hours or earning $580\". The error comes from the source's own wording (Georgetown), and the peg's \"97% passed the income test\" copies it. Georgetown itself says it does not know whether these were verified from data.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F13 percentages: the state's own printed shares (36.9% qualifying, 61.1% exempt, 1.9% hardship) do not reproduce from its counts. Against 7,280 they are 33.6% / 57.3% / 1.7%; against the 6,747 approvals 36.3% / 61.8% / 1.9%. The error is in the state document; the \"61.1%/36.9%\" finder figures came from it. KFF's 57%/34% are the counts over 7,280. Use counts, or 57%/34%/2%/7% of 7,280.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F13 exemption detail: 1,612 medically frail and 1,307 caregivers of a child 13 or under (sub-shares 38.6% and 31.3% of 4,171 do reproduce). Of the 127 hardships, 109 were \"Emergency Declaration\" (county disaster).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U81/F13 applications May–Aug 2026: 9,614 processed; 4,991 denied for any reason (51.9%); 557 work-rule denials (466 non-response, 83.7%; 91 non-compliant, 16.3%); 4,434 denied for other reasons. Work-rule denials = 557/4,991 = 11.2% of all denials; 557/4,089 subject applicants = 13.6% (KFF's 14%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW TRAP: the minutes carry a SECOND \"92%\", on the application side: 1,197 of 1,862 applicants who met qualifying activities did so by working or $580 \"~92%\". But 1,197/1,862 = 64.3%, and the line is mislabelled \"renewal approvals\" under applications. The state document is internally inconsistent here, and KFF relays it (\"the vast majority of people (92%)\"). Do not use the applicant-side 92%. Never let it be confused with the 92% non-response figure.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U102/F13: DHHS's answer on why people did not respond. The dossier's quoted wording \"too early to tell\" is a paraphrase (engine/KFF relay). The state's words are \"too early to determine trends in the data\". DHHS also cautions that the figures are not tied to the application or renewal month.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U30/F13 SFY25 baseline (all Medicaid categories, July 2024–June 2025): 349,242 renewals; 100,190 closures, 55% (54,613) procedural; ex parte rate \"estimated\" at 34% from a system proxy. 54,613/349,242 = 15.6% of renewals. Caveat: the report itself gives both 100,190 and 100,193 closures, counts repeat people, and mixes every category.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U78: Nebraska's data-first design (May 2026 deck): review existing data first; expects many people to need no extra paperwork; claims data can deem the medically frail exempt with no action; 30 days to respond; one month of compliance in a 12-month period. Narrowing: the no-documents declaration form (IDF) covers exemptions and non-work activities ONLY. Work hours or income that data cannot confirm need \"primary source documents\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G5 legal authority, Nebraska: the minutes record that CMS approved Nebraska's work-requirement SPA on 2026-09-04 with a \"companion letter\", which Nebraska is answering. That is four months after enforcement began on 2026-05-01. The minutes do not give the SPA number or say what the companion letter asks.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G5 staffing: asked whether staff will grow for 6-month renewals, DHHS said no. From 2027 the work rule is checked in one month of every 6 rather than one month a year (the dossier's 12-month caveat stands).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G5 call centre (secondary): officials told Tradeoffs/NPR the average wait is 10 minutes; Tradeoffs hit long waits and a dropped call; the Medicaid director said he would watch appeal counts. Advocates said some people waited up to 90 minutes (Advisory Board relaying Nebraska Examiner).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G5 pre-rule call-centre baseline (SFY25, all Medicaid): 87% of client calls answered, 13% abandoned, average wait 6 minutes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G5 appeals: no fair-hearing request or outcome count for Nebraska's work rule appears in any DHHS document read: the 5-21, 7-16 and 9-17 MAC materials, the annual report and the WorkRequirements page. Montana publishes none either. Montana's FAQ only says appeals follow the Notice of Adverse Action. Hearing reversals, the nearest evidence on whether non-responders were eligible, are unpublished.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G15 DISCREPANCY: Advisory Board (2026-09-24) relays a DHHS spokesperson's figure of 643 expansion enrollees losing coverage July 1–Aug 30, against the MAC minutes' 533 renewal denials for July–August. The windows and definitions are not reconcilable from what is published. Use the MAC document's counts and never mix the two. An earlier spokesperson figure: 65 renewals denied as of July 19 (WOWT/Nebraska Examiner).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G15 status of the document: it is a DRAFT, still carrying an unedited staff aside, and is due for approval at the 2026-11-12 meeting. No final version exists today. Recheck on or after 11-12; the July draft was approved with no edits.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G15/G7: no share of renewals cleared by data with no action by the person is published. DHHS says its IT is still being finished to report CMS metrics, then it will build a public dashboard. So \"most were verified automatically\" cannot be said.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U79/U103/F14: Montana reviewed people July–September 2026 with no denials or removals; from October 2026 people may be denied or removed. Re-fetched 2026-11-02: identical to the peg-hunt save, with no counts added.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U103/F14: Montana tries existing data first (\"ex parte\"), then requires forms (Appendix A and B) plus documents covering three months of the review period (not necessarily consecutive).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U79: Montana credits income below $580 as hours (income ÷ minimum wage) and lets people top up with volunteering, school or other activities to reach 80.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G5 legal authority, Montana: DPHHS filed an 1115 waiver in September 2025 and, at CMS's direction, converted it to a State Plan Amendment (DPHHS June 2026 release; read from the copy the peg hunt saved).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G15 Montana counts: none found. The FAQ and changes pages (fetched 11-02) carry no review-period or October counts. Two guessed HELP Act quarterly-report URLs (QE 06-30-26 and 09-30-26) returned 404, and a search found no October release. Say \"Montana has published no numbers yet\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U56/F14 Arkansas 63,792: secondary-verified only. The Democrat-Gazette story (Texarkana Gazette reprint, 2026-07-21; body paywalled, headline and lede read) attributes the count to a DHS spokesman. No DHS table, release or report gives it: the DHS work-requirement page via r.jina.ai, its soft-launch release and its statistical-reports page were checked. These were soft-launch status notices, not removals.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F14/U56 Arkansas: no penalties before 2027-01-01, at DHS's own page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U56/F14 Arkansas: no one loses coverage under the new rule in 2026, and DHS estimates about 42,000 (nearly 20%) of ARHOME will lose coverage. Both are a DHS spokesperson's and DHS's words relayed by the Arkansas Advocate (2026-07-06); the 42,000 is not in a DHS document I read. About 210,000 were enrolled in ARHOME in May.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e26"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G7 Arkansas: DHS's automated check uses wage data, SNAP/TEA records and claims and diagnosis data (spokesperson, relayed). After 2026, anyone it cannot verify gets 30 days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e27"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U84/F14 Iowa: the requirement starts 2026-12-01 for certain IHAWP members; the first renewal cohort is February 2027; 6-month renewals begin with renewals due March 2027; the exclusions include caregivers of a child under 14.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e28"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U85 Georgia: proposed Pathways amendment asks for a 90% match, citing exclusions now imposed by federal law. Cost $227.5M in SFY2027 and $1.2057B in SFY2028; written comment before 2026-09-14. I did not search for a CMS decision.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e29"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U36/F24 South Dakota Amendment I: the duty to cover expansion adults runs only until the federal match falls below 90%; the Attorney General's explanation says it ends the expanded coverage then.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e30"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U107: the enrolled text keeps the existing clause letting the state impose a work requirement (with CMS permission) on non-disabled expansion adults. Amendment I adds no work requirement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e31"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U54/F24: sponsors say it is not automatic and the AG says it is; more than 31,000 on expansion (DSS dashboard, relayed); about $72M if the federal share fell to 70%. The result is due 11-03 and was NOT checked (it is tomorrow).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e32"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U55/F24: nine automatic-trigger states (KFF, 2024), with Arizona's at 80%, plus three mitigation-only states. The Ohio 2025 addition and Utah HB15 were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e33"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G7 ANSWERED from the rule itself: CMS allows income as a proxy for hours BECAUSE data sources such as quarterly wage data carry earnings, not hours. It concedes that not all work can be verified from financial data and that returned data may lack hours. States must also use payroll data \"available to the State\". So \"data can test 80 hours\" is really \"data can test earnings\"; hours come from income ÷ minimum wage or from paperwork.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e34"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G7: the federal Hub matching notice that now covers community-engagement checks lists income \"based on Federal Tax Information\", VA disability ratings, school enrollment and SSA benefits. It lists no hours, payroll or monthly wage source.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e35"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G7 Nebraska's own statement: work (hours or income) must be verified, and if data cannot do it the person supplies primary-source documents (hours, income, self-employment). Verification \"will change\" as data sources become available. In July DHHS was taking self-attestation for medically frail.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e36"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"G7 Nebraska, July: medically frail accepted on attestation; DHHS confirmed terminations had begun.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e37"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U34/F20 CMS vendor pledges (page 403 to direct fetch; read through r.jina.ai): Deloitte $2M per state fixed fee; Gainwell $2M plus $50 per member per year for those it verifies; Accenture no-cost to partner states; Maximus no development, licensing or maintenance cost through 2028 for existing E&E clients (deployment discounted, not free). The figure \"600\" appears nowhere on the page: drop the \"$600M+\". Conduent's terms were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e38"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U50/F20 state IT prices (KFF Health News via CPA Practice Advisor; the underlying DocumentCloud estimates were not opened): at least $45.6M across five states; Wisconsin nearly $6M; Iowa at least $20M; Vermont about $1.8M of $5M; Illinois at least $12M. Narrowing: Kentucky's $1.6M is \"initial changes\", and the linked change requests include SNAP changes, so it is not Medicaid-work-only. Illinois estimates up to 360,000 could lose Medicaid.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e39"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U51/F20 Colorado: opened at the state auditor's own report (September 2023). It found at least one problem in 72 of 80 sampled system letters (90%). \"Problem\" includes clarity and completeness, not only factual error, so do not say \"90% were wrong\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e40"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U92/F20 Deloitte's footprint: KFF Health News's own 2024 review says 25 states and \"at least $5 billion\"; Healthcare Dive (2025) says 25 states and \"at least $6 billion\", citing a later KFF investigation. Gainwell has 30 states, per its own website. Say \"25 states, contracts worth billions\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e41"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U51/F20 Florida new mothers: the senators' letter as reported.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e42"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U100/F20 Maryland: abandonment 37.7% in August 2023 and about one in four in September, with staffing concerns at the call centre and Maximus working on staffing plans. The \"16 states, 25-minute average wait, 29% hang-up\" figure is NOT in this source and was not found: drop it or source it to CMS.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e43"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U93 managed care: the rule lets states use plans for outreach and education and to share enrollee data (frailty, treatment) with the state, and says some activities are prohibited to plans. I did not locate the exact text that bars plans from deciding compliance (SHVS, the dossier's source, is secondary). Outreach notices go out in the 4th–6th month before the effective date, through at least two modalities.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e44"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"F14 good-faith delay: initial exemptions run at most 6 months and all expire by 2028-12-31. The plaintiff states' delay request and CMS's answer were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e45"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Verified-sample relay check: KFF's body text on the 533 matches the state's counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e46"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Summary/abstract-style wording check: KFF's key-takeaway bullet says 7% \"lost coverage due to not meeting work requirements\". Wrong: 490 of the 533 were never found non-compliant, only non-responsive. Right wording: \"7% lost coverage under the work rule, most for not answering a verification request\". The error is the source's own summary wording; the body text (E46) is right.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-e47"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1 (G: who is outside): only the ACA 'adult group' (expansion adults) can be subject under the state plan; every other Medicaid group is outside, e.g. the parents-and-caretakers group and the optional group above 133% FPL. Plus some section 1115 waiver adults 19–64. One plain line: 'it applies only to adults 19 to 64 who got Medicaid through the expansion'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1: the adult group itself is defined to leave out people on Medicare and people in other mandatory groups, naming people eligible because they get SSI. So an SSI recipient is not an expansion adult and the rule doesn't reach them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, SSA's side: in most states an SSI award brings Medicaid automatically (the SSI application doubles as the Medicaid application). In the others the person applies separately, so say 'in most states'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1: SSDI. SSDI brings Medicare only after 24 months of entitlement, and anyone entitled to Medicare Part A or enrolled in Part B is deemed to meet the requirement. So 'on SSDI and Medicare = outside' is right. 'On SSDI = exempt' is not the federal rule's wording: in the first 24 months a low-income SSDI recipient can be an expansion adult, and is spared through the frailty route (T1-6, T1-7), not by name. Medicare is a mandatory EXCEPTION (deemed compliant for the month), not an exclusion: same practical effect.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, the rule's own text for the Medicare and age exceptions (435.553): the state must deem compliant, for the month, anyone under 19, anyone on Medicare A or B, anyone in the listed mandatory groups, and anyone released from jail or prison in the past 3 months. 'Over 64' is outside because applicable individuals are 'under 65', and at 65 most people have Medicare anyway.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2: IS A PENDING DISABILITY CLAIM AN EXEMPTION? Not by itself. The rule's five frailty groups name no pending SSA claim, and a search of the rule for 'pending' finds only application-processing text. Group (A) borrows SSA's DEFINITION of disability (section 1614), not an SSA award. The statute leaves the finding to the state (T1-7). The state works from its own condition list and 12 months of the person's MEDICAL claims (paid, pended or denied: Medicaid claims, not SSA's), and must have a route for a condition not on the list. So a claimant can be excluded while SSA is still deciding, if the state's data or the person's statement or paperwork shows they fit a group. APPROXIMATE because the 'no award needed' reading is ours: the rule never says it in so many words.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2: the statute's five groups, and that the STATE determines who is in them (to the Secretary's standards). The ADL group carries its own statutory limit, a disability that 'significantly impairs' the ability to perform one or more activities of daily living. That limit is Congress's own and was NOT touched by the ruling. Never say 'every 'significantly impairs' test is gone'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, what changed on 10-29 (detail at A1–A5): the general test, that the condition 'significantly impairs' the ability to comply, is declared unlawful and vacated. Fitting one of the five groups is now enough. The look-back and the sworn-statement limit were upheld (A3).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, CMS's own guidance after the ruling: as of 2026-11-02 the agency's community-engagement page lists nothing on frailty after 10-29. Its newest frailty item is a slide deck posted September 8 (not opened: see header). Sayable: 'the agency hasn't published new frailty guidance since the ruling (as of November 2)'. ⏭ re-check before air.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, HOW IT IS PROVEN (435.557(f)): the state must first check claims and encounter data from the past 12 months, including pended or denied claims. Through 2027 the state MAY take documents or a statement under penalty of perjury, each time. That is a state OPTION, not a right. From 2028 a sworn statement is accepted only once per period of enrolment, then verified with data or documents at the next redetermination. Once verified, frailty is re-checked at least every 12 months. Overlaps A28 and A3; restated here for the disability script.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, Nebraska's form (May 2026 committee deck): claims are reviewed about 90 days before the end of eligibility, and a match means exempt with no action. With no claims, the person fills in the IDF form with basic information. No health records or doctor's note are needed, and applicants can self-declare. Overlaps E10 and E37; this is the state-form contrast with Montana (T1-12).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, Montana's form: from October 1, 2026 the state checks 12 months of Medicaid claims against its condition list. Without a match, EXISTING members must send a Provider Attestation Form, while NEW applicants may self-declare. A condition not on the list can be put forward on a request form with the person's provider. So Montana asks more of current members than the federal floor allows it to through 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2 trap: Montana's frailty page, as saved 2026-11-02, still prints the vacated 'significantly impairs … ability to comply' definition. It describes its list as conditions that show a person is 'not able to work', which is close to the standard the court struck. Montana does not appear in the saved filings of the Massachusetts suit, and the vacatur is not limited to the suing states on its face (A1). So the page is out of date, or else Montana reads the ruling as not binding on it; we can't tell which. Don't quote a state page as current law on frailty.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1/Q2: Montana's FAQ tells members that SSI and SSDI recipients, and 'people with disabilities', don't have to meet the requirement. For SSI (outside the group) and SSDI (Medicare, or frailty group A) that matches the federal rule in effect. 'People with disabilities' is wider than the rule's words: a disability is an exclusion only through the five frailty groups, or via Medicare or another eligibility group. Montana's plain-language gloss; never air it as the federal rule.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, SSA's own processing times (page last updated October 26, 2026; monthly averages read from its chart descriptions), September 2026: initial claims 185 days (209 a year earlier), reconsiderations 201 days (236), hearings 287 days (278: RISING). Hearings averaged 270 days over fiscal 2026. Our arithmetic, not SSA's: someone who goes through all three steps waits roughly 185 + 201 + 287 ≈ 670 days, about 22 months, of decision time alone. Filing and mailing gaps come on top. On air say 'about six months for a first decision, and well over a year more if you appeal', and label any total as our sum.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, the queue: pending first-step claims ROSE in the last year, from 885,000 to about 911,000. Pending hearings rose from about 281,000 to 358,000. Pending reconsiderations fell from 371,000 to about 297,000.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, the two SSA framings that look like a contradiction and aren't: the October 23 press release says the backlog FELL 23%, but it measures from fiscal 2024 (nearly 1.2 million → 911,000). The performance page's year-on-year count ROSE (T1-16). Same 911,000, different baselines; name the baseline if either is aired. SSA's FAQ, dated March 5, 2024, still says 6–8 months for a first decision. It is the older figure; use the performance page's 185 days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, MENTAL ILLNESS, in the rule's words: frailty group (C), 'a disabling mental disorder'. A non-disabling disorder doesn't qualify. The rule gives examples a state may treat as disabling: schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders, moderate or severe bipolar disorder, major depressive disorder, panic disorder. It can be temporary. The preamble's added direction that it also 'significantly impair' the ability to comply is the test the court struck (T1-8); 'disabling' is Congress's word and stays.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, SUBSTANCE USE, two separate exclusions: (B) frailty, a substance use disorder, except people in stable recovery for 5+ years; this route does NOT require being in treatment. And (8), anyone participating in a drug or alcohol treatment and rehabilitation program; states may set a minimum time commitment. Montana sets that at ASAM level 2.1 or above (intensive outpatient and up), per its FAQ.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q5, CARING FOR A DISABLED ADULT: yes, it exempts the caregiver. The exclusion covers a parent, guardian, caretaker relative or family caregiver of a child 13 or under OR of a 'disabled individual'. That person can be ANY age, an older adult included, and is disabled by the ADA definition. They need not be on Medicaid or any other disability programme.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q5, the caregiver's three routes, which confirm the brief's unverified engine summary (dossier finding 2). (A) lives with the person and gives regular help that is 'not solely incidental'. (B) a relative living elsewhere who gives regular help. (C) a non-relative living elsewhere, who needs at least 80 hours of help a month. The state may not require the cared-for person's name.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q5, how Montana checks it: its data match covers caregivers of a dependent CHILD only, so a caregiver of a disabled adult must send the Self-Declaration Form. Nebraska's comparable form wasn't readable (header).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U103 / brief's unverified 'engine summary' (dossier finding 6): COVERAGE DURING THE CURE. Medicaid continues until the person is found ineligible. After the 30 days the state must check every other basis of eligibility, and disenrolment can come no later than the end of the month after the 30 days end, after advance notice and hearing rights. Confirms the dossier's wording (A32 had checked only the 30 + 5 days).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U103: THE RECONSIDERATION ROUTE. A person enrolled on income rules (MAGI), disenrolled for not sending what the notice asked for, who sends it within 90 days (or longer if the state allows) must be reconsidered. The papers are treated AS AN APPLICATION, so the person must show engagement, or an exception, in the month before. A frail or caregiving person can show their exclusion instead (435.558(c)(1)(ii)). The dossier's unchecked '90-day length' is confirmed. It comes from the renewal rule 435.916(a)(3)(iii), which 435.558(f) applies here.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"U104 as the brief uses it (reapplication; note the dossier's own U104 is the SNAP/TANF point, not checked here): NO LOCK-OUT. States may not restrict reapplying, or coverage once found eligible again, because of an earlier denial or disenrolment. The noncompliance notice must say how to reapply.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (PERM headline): the latest official Medicaid improper-payment rate I found is FY2025: 6.12% ($37.39B), a rolling three-cycle figure (reviews in 2023–2025, about 17 states each), up from 5.09% in FY2024. CMS itself says 77% of it is insufficient documentation, \"generally not indicative of fraud\", and that the rate is not a fraud measure. Assumption, unverified: the FY2026 rate is normally published in HHS's financial report in mid-November; ⏭ re-check after November 15.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (eligibility share): of the 6.12 points, the eligibility component is 4.42 points (fee-for-service 4.60, managed care 0.00; the overall is a weighted sum, so the components don't add). CMS flags that FY2025 includes the unwinding (April 2023 to December 2025). The single-cycle 2025 figure (2.30% overall, 1.17% eligibility) covers only that cycle's 17 states: never air it as national.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (what \"improper\" means): CMS's cause table for FY2025 federal improper payments. \"Insufficient information to determine eligibility\" is $16.74B (44.8%); payments where the person was actually ineligible for the program or service are $6.24B (16.7%). The definition of the biggest code says the person \"may be\" eligible, with the paperwork missing. Fair on air: \"most of Medicaid's improper payments are missing paperwork; about a sixth were for people found ineligible.\"","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (expansion adults specifically): CMS's eligibility-review table by category puts the expansion (\"newly eligible\") group at 5.54% of federal payments (95% CI 4.05–7.03%), against 4.93% for all MAGI groups, 4.68% for children and 4.86% for parents. The intervals overlap; this is a payment-weighted error rate (paperwork gaps included), not a share of people who are ineligible.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (FY2024 ineligible-person dollars, relayed by GAO): of about $31B improper in FY2024, HHS put $4.9B on people not eligible for the program or service. Consistent with T2-3's FY2025 $6.24B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (the PERM trend trap): the 2019–2020 spike (rolling 21.36% in 2020) followed CMS's revised eligibility procedures, which OIG says \"may have factored into\" the high rates. Never air \"improper payments fell from 21% to 6%\" as an achievement or \"rose to 21%\" as a crisis without the method change.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (HHS-OIG on expansion eligibility, four states): OIG's 2022 roll-up of audits of New York, California, Colorado and Kentucky, covering 2014–2015. 109 of 460 sampled expansion adults \"did not meet or may not have met\" the rules; about $1.4B federal for more than 700,000 ineligible or potentially ineligible expansion adults. Two things the supporters' use of it leaves out: the same audits estimate more than $5B for almost 5 million traditional (non-expansion) enrollees; and the causes named are state \"human and system errors\" and missing procedures, not enrollee fraud. \"Potentially ineligible\" means the state couldn't document it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (OIG California, the largest single finding): sample of 150 expansion adults, October 2014 to March 2015; 38 ineligible or potentially ineligible; estimated $628.8M federal share for 366,078 ineligible plus $402.4M for 79,055 potentially ineligible. A 2014–15 snapshot, the first year of expansion.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (OIG Louisiana, the counter-example): sample of 120 expansion adults, July–December 2016: 115 met the rules, 4 did not, 1 may not have. $20.1M (all federal) for an estimated 16,358 people. The cause was analysts not following procedures; OIG cut its estimate after the state's documents. Audits vary widely by state and year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (CMS duplicate coverage, July 17, 2025): 2024 data, monthly averages: 1.2 million in Medicaid/CHIP in two or more states, and 1.6 million in both Medicaid/CHIP and a subsidised marketplace plan; \"potentially\"; about $14B a year in savings claimed. All Medicaid/CHIP enrollees (children included), not expansion adults. The release describes the analysis only as engineers examining enrollment data; no method or margin is given. Label it \"the agency's own estimate\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (GAO on cross-state duplicates, published November 17, 2025): six states (CA, GA, NY, PA, TN, TX), fiscal 2023, counting only 3+ consecutive months of overlap: about 149,000 people in Medicaid/CHIP in two of those states, out of about 32.6 million Medicaid SSNs reviewed; $379M+ in duplicate capitation. GAO calls the sums \"relatively small\" against enrolment but significant, and partly due to the pandemic's continuous-enrolment rule. Never set beside CMS's 1.2 million as a contradiction: six states vs all, 2023 vs 2024, a three-month buffer vs a monthly average.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (GAO on Medicaid plus marketplace): over $1B in marketplace tax credits for about 340,000 people also in Medicaid/CHIP in the six states (3+ months), fiscal 2023. GAO notes it may be the Medicaid enrolment, not the tax credit, that was improper (e.g., someone who moved).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (duplicates are fixed by a DIFFERENT section of the law): CMS's November 6, 2025 bulletin ties the duplicate-enrolment duties to section 71103 (address checks from January 1, 2027; a national system), not the work requirement (section 71119). So \"the work requirement removes duplicate enrollees\" is wrong as worded; the law's duplicate fix is separate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Extends C31 (the rule's stated purposes, its own words): escaping \"isolation and dependency\" and self-sufficiency; alignment with SNAP and TANF; \"a short-term hand up\"; prioritising the most vulnerable. Its program-integrity aim is about making STATE determinations auditable with data, not about a count of ineligible people.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Extends A17 (the rule's only link between the work rule and improper enrolment): in its coverage-loss model, the agency EXPECTS that improperly enrolled people and people with other coverage won't return paperwork. It gives no count of improper enrollees anywhere in the full text (one hit for \"improperly\"; no improper-payment or error rate cited). It is an assumption, not a measured finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (PERM is the rule's own audit tool): the preamble names PERM audits as how CMS will catch states that grant the frailty exemption too freely, and uses \"program integrity\" for the 80-hour caregiver threshold. The integrity worry runs both ways: against wrong exemptions as well as wrong enrolment.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (the House report's stated purpose, H. Rept. 119-106, Budget Committee, May 2025, Medicaid title): the work provision sits under \"Increasing Personal Accountability\"; its rationale is that health care and work are linked (employer coverage, Medicare earned by work, veterans) and that it will \"promote the dignity of work\". This is the House bill (section 44141), legislative history, not the enacted text.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (the same report keeps ineligibility as a SEPARATE problem): its ineligible-enrollee fixes are duplicate enrolment, deceased enrollees and immigration-status checks, plus provider taxes; the work requirement is argued on accountability, not on removing ineligible people. Script: supporters' integrity case and their work case are two different arguments in their own documents. What's corrected: the framing \"the work requirement is how the law tackles ineligible enrolment\". Right wording: \"the law tackles ineligible and duplicate enrolment mainly through other provisions; supporters argue the work rule on accountability.\" Where the error came from: the source's own blending in supporters' statements (Paragon's IFC statement, C29), which the steelman relayed.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Paragon, August 2026, a supporters' think tank; ADVOCACY): \"nearly half\" (46%, 9.2 million) of expansion enrollees in 2024 \"likely ineligible\", $32.9B federal. It's a residual model (enrolment minus a survey-based count of eligible people times an ASSUMED take-up rate), not an audit. Its own range runs from 5.2 million (27%+, 100% take-up, \"implausible\") to 11.5 million (55% take-up); a 150% FPL buffer gives 8.2 million. Right wording: \"a supporters' think tank estimates, from survey data and an assumed sign-up rate, between a quarter and over half\". What was wrong: the title and press wording (\"nearly half … likely do not meet eligibility requirements\") present one assumption's result as a finding. Where it came from: the source's own wording, relayed by secondary press.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Paragon against the official measures): the paper itself says 46% is higher than the federal audits, and explains the gap by audit age and coverage. Against CMS's 5.54% payment error rate for the expansion group (T2-4) the measures differ (people vs dollars; model vs case review), so neither refutes the other; but no official audit finds anything near half.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (the method critique, for balance): a 2019 Health Affairs Forefront piece on the earlier survey-based method (same co-author family) says unadjusted self-reported survey income is not Medicaid's taxable-income test and household rules differ, and that it \"should not be construed as an audit\". This is about the 2019-era work; the 2026 paper adds a 150% FPL buffer in response. No published critique of the 2026 paper was found.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Paragon's own remedies omit the work rule): the paper's recommendations are cutting the 90% match, tighter income checks, ending passive renewals and annual PERM audits; it credits the law's six-month renewals, duplicate checks and 2030 error-rate penalties. Zero hits for \"work requirement\" or \"community engagement\" in the paper. So supporters' improper-enrolment evidence does not, in their own paper, argue for the work rule. (Paragon's IFC statement does tie weaker verification to improper enrolment, C29.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (FGA, April 14, 2025, ADVOCACY: what the savings are FOR): its purpose statement is moving \"able-bodied adults\" to work, saving taxpayers money, \"preserve resources for the truly needy\", and shoring up the Medicare and Social Security trust funds. Its $260B over 2025–2034 is FGA's own extrapolation from a 2022 CBO score of a narrower plan ($135B over nine years): not a CBO estimate of this law. Never pair with CBO's $326B (A10).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (FGA's \"truly needy\" premise): FGA says expansion adults push \"more than 700,000\" disabled people \"to the back of the line\" on waiting lists, and that about 22,000 died waiting. The 22,000 was not traced to origin: off air.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (FGA's 700,000 at its origin, KFF, October 31, 2024): the count is real (over 710,000 on waiting or interest lists in 2024), but KFF says most are in states that don't screen for eligibility first; most are eligible for other Medicaid home care meanwhile; the lists predate expansion and exist in expansion and non-expansion states. So \"700,000 on waiting lists\" is sayable; \"expansion adults put them there\" is FGA's argument, not shown by the count. Where the error came from: the source's own wording (FGA), which cites the count as proof of displacement.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, the whole law): CBO's official score of P.L. 119-21, against its January 2025 baseline, FY2025–2034, in millions: total outlays −$1,091,345M (spending down about $1.1 trillion), total revenues −$4,485,789M (revenue down about $4.5 trillion), net deficit +$3,394,444M (about $3.4 trillion added to deficits). CBO adds that deficits keep rising after 2034. Third-party copy of CBO's own PDF (cbo.gov refused); the figures match CRS's relay of the same table (T3-3) and the $3.4T in search snippets of cbo.gov.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, the tax side): CBO's table has no single \"tax provisions\" line. Title VII (Senate Finance) holds the tax provisions and also the Medicaid and health ones. Its revenues fall $4,521,327M; the revenue figures are JCT's. Title VII's own outlays fall $941,720M, because the Medicaid cuts sit in the same title. Fair on air: \"about $4.5 trillion less revenue, mostly from the tax changes\". Never \"the tax cuts cost exactly $4.5 trillion\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, the Medicaid share): CRS, relaying CBO's July 21, 2025 table: health provisions cut federal outlays by $1.1T; the Medicaid provisions alone by $989.7B; the work provision by $325.6B (= A10). Arithmetic (mine): Medicaid's $989.7B is about 91% of the law's net $1.09T spending cut (the law also adds spending on defense and the border) and about 22% of the $4.49T revenue loss; the work provision alone is about 7% of it. CRS's Medicaid figure includes the nursing-home staffing moratorium. SECONDARY-VERIFIED (CRS; CBO's detail table not openable).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, a competing total + Q3b): KFF puts total Medicaid cuts at $911B, against CRS's $989.7B. The gap is scope (which provisions count as \"Medicaid\"); I didn't reconcile it. Never air both; say \"nearly a trillion dollars\" (CRS/CBO) or \"about $900 billion\" (KFF) and cite one. KFF also says the work provision's savings \"largely stem from coverage losses\". That is KFF's characterisation; T3-15 is the primary for it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, the procedural link): the FY2025 budget resolution (H. Con. Res. 14, agreed April 10, 2025) told House Energy and Commerce (Medicaid's House committee) to cut deficits by at least $880B over 2025–2034, and allowed Ways and Means (taxes) to add up to $4.5T. Section 4001 ties the two. If the spending committees' cuts came to less than $2T, the $4.5T tax allowance shrank by the shortfall; if they came to more, it grew by the excess. That's a documented procedural link between the spending cuts and the room for tax cuts. It is a rule in the budget resolution, not a stated motive.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, the Senate side): the enacted law is the Senate's version, where the tax and Medicaid provisions both sit with Senate Finance. The same resolution let Finance's changes raise deficits by up to $1.5T. This is the resolution's wording; how the Senate scored against that ceiling (its own baseline) is not verified here. Don't air it; it's context for why \"the cuts paid for the taxes\" has no single official ledger behind it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, the supporters' own framing): the House Budget Committee's report presents the resolution as cutting spending by at least $1.5T and blames deficits on spending. CBO told E&C its Medicaid-heavy title met the $880B target. Steelman: the majority framed the cuts as deficit reduction and spending restraint, not as paying for tax cuts. Both are true as arithmetic (T3-8).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1 WORDING: \"the Medicaid cuts pay for the tax cuts\" is WRONG as arithmetic. CBO's score has revenues down $4.49T and spending down $1.09T, so the law adds $3.39T to deficits. The cuts offset about a quarter of the revenue loss (24%; Medicaid alone about 22%). Right wording: \"partly offsets\" (or \"covers part of the cost of\"). \"Sits in the same law as\" is true but too weak, given section 4001's explicit link (T3-5). Money is fungible, so no dollar of Medicaid savings is earmarked for any particular tax provision. Error source: the viewer framing / the common political shorthand.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t3-8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q1, colour): the CMS rule that implements the work requirement calls the law by an agency name built around the tax cut (\"Working Families Tax Cut legislation\"). It's a fact about the record, usable on screen. Don't read it as motive.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q2, the enacted law, CBO Aug 11, 2025): average per year over 2026–2034, in 2025 dollars, against CBO's January 2025 baseline. The lowest tenth loses about $1,200 a year (3.1% of income), mainly from Medicaid and SNAP. The highest tenth gains about $13,600 a year (2.7%), mainly from lower taxes. The middle (5th and 6th tenths) gains $800 and $1,200. On average, household resources rise.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q2, what CBO counts): yes, the Medicaid cuts are in it. CBO allocates taxes (JCT's distribution), Medicaid, SNAP, states' responses and public goods. Net channels: taxes and cash transfers +$3.3T; in-kind transfers (mainly Medicaid, SNAP) −$900B. Of the Medicaid change, CBO puts 47% on beneficiaries and 53% on providers and insurers. Footnote 8 names the work-required adults as one of the two main ways the eligibility changes hit low-income households. Excluded: some tax provisions (estate tax and others), debt service, macro effects. Deciles are ranked by income after transfers and taxes, adjusted for household size.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q2, the House-passed bill, CBO June 12, 2025): lowest tenth −$1,600 a year (3.9%); highest tenth +$12,000 (2.3%). This is for H.R. 1 as passed by the House on May 22, 2025, NOT the enacted law. It's superseded by T3-10; never air it as the law's effect. Copy of CBO's letter hosted by a county government's mail service (cbo.gov refused).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3a (section 71106, the law's text): the provision exists, but it is NOT a new penalty. It amends the existing 3% error-rate penalty in section 1903(u) of the Social Security Act. It limits how much the Secretary may waive, adds \"insufficient information to confirm eligibility\" payments to the errors that count, requires the reduction \"to the extent practicable\", and applies from fiscal year 2030. Right wording: \"the law tightens an old penalty on states whose eligibility errors top 3%, starting in 2030\". Error source: the question's own framing (\"a separate provision penalising…\"). The House version would have removed the good-faith waiver outright (House report §44107); the enacted text caps it instead.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t3-13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q3a, CBO's savings for it): CRS's background confirms the 3% penalty and its good-faith waiver are prior law. The change starts October 1, 2029. CBO: section 71106 cuts federal outlays by $7.55B over FY2025–2034 (revenues −$0.335B). For comparison, section 71103 (duplicate enrolment across states) is scored at $17.4B. Both together are about $25B over ten years, against $37.39B improper in one year (T2-1). SECONDARY-VERIFIED (CRS relaying CBO's July 21, 2025 table).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q3b, where the work rule's savings come from: the agency's own method): CMS computes the savings as people who leave the rolls times the average cost per enrollee ($350.3B federal over 10 years; = A-pass figure). It is not a reduction in improper payments. CMS does name improperly enrolled people as one reason some won't return paperwork, but it doesn't count them. It also assumes about half of procedural losses (about 5.5 points) are people who DO meet the rules. No official source I read says the requirement's savings come from cutting improper payments.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q3b, the House report's rationale): the committee lists the program-integrity measures (duplicate enrolment, deceased enrollees, citizenship checks) as the answer to \"ineligible enrollees\". It gives the work requirement a different reason: personal accountability and \"the dignity of work\". The supporters' own report doesn't sell the work rule as an improper-payments fix either.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q3c, comparator): CMS's FY2025 table: $37,386.79M improper out of $610,988.02M in projected federal Medicaid payments, i.e. 6.12%, or about 6 cents of every federal Medicaid dollar. Arithmetic (mine): missing eligibility paperwork ($16.74B) is about 2.7 cents; payments for people or services found ineligible ($6.24B) are about 1 cent. Same domain, same unit, sourced. Second comparator (from T3-14): the law's penalty on state error rates is scored at $7.55B over ten years, about a fifth of one year's improper payments.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q3d, recoveries): I found NO official national figure for Medicaid improper payments recovered. CMS's own payment-integrity scorecard (Q3 2026) leaves its recovery measure blank. It says eligibility overpayments can be recovered only through the PERM sample, and other errors only \"on a sample basis\". So most of the $37B is an estimate projected from a sample, not a bill anyone can collect. The scorecard's FY2025 overpayment figure is $35,030M (5.73% of outlays). That is overpayments only, from a different sampling window; never set it beside the $37.39B.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"NEW (Q3d, the nearest official recovery figure, a DIFFERENT measure): the state Medicaid Fraud Control Units recovered about $2B in FY2025 ($1.3B criminal, $706M civil). These are recoveries from provider-fraud and patient-abuse cases, federal and state combined, not recovered improper payments. On air only as \"fraud prosecutors recovered about $2 billion last year\", never as \"the share of the $37B recovered\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, the expected primary: GAO-21-45. CORRECTED date: it is OCTOBER 2020 (cover; GAO-26-108703 footnote 1 dates it Oct. 19, 2020), not November 2020 (the error was in this top-up's own order). Census data: the 2019 CPS, covering 2018. 12 million wage-earning Medicaid adults aged 19–64; about 70% worked 35+ hours a week. Employer names: February 2020 data from 15 agencies (6 Medicaid, 9 SNAP) in 11 states. No single employer held more than 4% of working enrollees in any state.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t4-1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, the CURRENT primary: GAO-26-108703, dated June 22, 2026, at the request of the Senate HELP ranking member, revised August 24, 2026 (a Rhode Island table fix). The same 15 agencies in 11 states, with September 2025 state data. The six Medicaid states that gave employer data are Georgia, Indiana, Maine, Massachusetts, Oklahoma and Rhode Island (Table 6). GAO says the employer estimates hold for those states only, not for the nation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, full-time share (GAO, national CPS 2025 = calendar 2024): 66.1% of wage-earning adult Medicaid enrollees worked 35+ hours a week, against 87.0% of non-enrollees; 47.8% worked full-time for 50–52 weeks. POPULATION: ALL adult Medicaid enrollees 19–64 with wage income, in ALL states, parents included, self-employed EXCLUDED. That is not the population the rule reaches (expansion adults, D1–D12), so don't present it as \"people subject to the rule\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, KFF's figure (CPS March 2024 = calendar 2023; Medicaid adults 19–64 not on SSI/SSDI/Medicare): 69% of WORKING Medicaid adults worked full-time. This agrees with GAO's 66% within survey noise; they are two cuts of the same survey, not independent confirmations. KFF also shows a full-time, full-year minimum-wage job ($12,688) is under the expansion limit, which is why full-time workers can qualify.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, industries (GAO, 2024): 70.6% of wage-earning Medicaid adults were in five industry groups. Compared with non-enrollees they are over-represented in arts/entertainment/recreation plus accommodation and food services (16.6% vs 7.5%) and in wholesale and retail trade (15.5% vs 12.1%). The largest single group is education, health care and social services (21.8%, slightly UNDER non-enrollees' 24.3%). Figures read from Table 3; the paired non-enrollee numbers sit on separate lines of the extract.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, the EMPLOYERS (September 2025, six states). The top 25 employers together held from 5.5% (Rhode Island) to 30.9% (Georgia) of working enrollees who had an employer on file. Number 1 was Walmart in Georgia, Indiana and Oklahoma; Hannaford in Maine; the Commonwealth of Massachusetts (a PUBLIC employer) in Massachusetts; and Stop & Shop in Rhode Island. The largest single share anywhere is Walmart in Georgia, about 4.8% (Table 13). Across all 11 states, 46 companies were top-25 in at least two states, and 17 of those were among the 50 largest Fortune 500 companies by headcount. The 2020 line \"no employer above 4%\" no longer holds for Georgia.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, the other side, from the SAME GAO report (national, 2024): wage-earning Medicaid adults are LESS likely than other workers to work for very large employers (1,000+ staff: 35.2% vs 48.2% of non-enrollees). They are MORE likely to work for small ones (under 10 staff: 21.1% vs 12.5%; 10–24 staff: 19.3% vs 14.1%). So about 40% work at firms under 25 staff. Non-enrollee figures read from Table 5's paired lines.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, why low-wage jobs often come without affordable cover (KFF, 2023): 46% of Medicaid workers are at firms under 25 staff. Those firms are not subject to the ACA's penalty for failing to offer cover. 46% are in agriculture and service industries. Fewer than 4 in 10 part-time workers were eligible for job-based cover, and an offer can still be unaffordable.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, GAO's own note on the employer mandate: large employers must offer qualifying cover to FULL-TIME workers (30+ hours on average) or risk a penalty, and owe nothing for part-timers. That is why part-time status matters to the \"employers keep them on Medicaid\" reading.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-9"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1, GAO's CAUTION, what the numbers don't mean. (a) The state data name the employer of record, not necessarily the current one. (b) The employer estimates hold only for the reporting states. (c) GAO draws no conclusion about any employer's wages or benefits, and the body makes no recommendation. (c) is our reading: a search of both reports for \"recommend\" finds only GAO's boilerplate. So GAO's tables show WHERE enrollees work. They do not show that a named employer pays less, or offers less, than its competitors.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-10"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, THE LINE in dollars: the 2026 HHS guideline for one person (48 states + DC) is $15,960. 138% of it is $22,025 a year, about $1,835 a month (our arithmetic). Alaska and Hawaii have higher guidelines. KFF's pages still quote the 2025 figure, $21,597 for one person. NOT checked here: the date each state's Medicaid program switched to the 2026 figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-11"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, STATUS OF THE ENHANCED CREDITS as of late October 2026 (KFF, published Oct 30, 2026): they EXPIRED after 2025 and the federal government has not restored them. Some state exchanges backfill: New Mexico fully; California for 138–165% FPL, widening to 165–200% for 2027. Not checked at Congress.gov or IRS for a bill after January 2026; KFF's Oct 30 brief is the latest read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-12"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, what the enhanced credits had done, and how they ended (ASTHO, Jan 20, 2026): under ARPA and then the IRA, the share of income owed started at 0% at low incomes and was capped at 8.5%. The Senate's December 2025 votes all failed, so the schedule reverted on Jan 1, 2026. (The \"0% up to 150% FPL\" band is general knowledge; this page says only \"starting at 0% for low incomes\".)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-13"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, AN EXAMPLE PREMIUM (label it an example; our arithmetic on IRS's 2026 table). 2026 coverage uses the 2025 guideline ($15,650), so 140% FPL = $21,910. The table runs 3.14% to 4.19% across 133–150%. Interpolated at 140%, that is about 3.57%: about $780 a year, or about $65 a month, for the BENCHMARK (second-cheapest silver) plan, whatever its sticker price. Under the enhanced schedule the same person paid $0 for it. Cheaper plans can cost less than $65; their deductibles were not checked. The 2027 table (a later Rev. Proc.) was not fetched.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-14"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, cross-check of T4-14's method on KFF's own example: a 45-year-old on $20,000 (128% FPL) in a NON-expansion state goes from $0 to $420 a year for the benchmark plan. That is exactly 2.10% of $20,000, the IRS bottom row, so our method matches KFF's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-15"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, CLIFF OR SLOPE? For PREMIUMS it is a STEP, then a slope. Below 138% in an expansion state you are on Medicaid at little or no premium (and can't get credits, being Medicaid-eligible). Just above it you pay about 3.5% of income for the benchmark plan, and that share climbs with income. The step reappeared when the enhanced credits lapsed. Our reading from the IRS table (T4-14). Two more points. Medicaid counts MONTHLY income while credits use ANNUAL income, so someone can be eligible for both at once. And the uncapped repayment (T4-17) raises the cost of guessing income wrong. Deductibles and cost-sharing above 138% were not checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-16"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, DOES THE 2025 LAW CHANGE MARKETPLACE HELP NEAR THE LINE? It does not move the 100%/138% eligibility points. It does: (1) end credits for plans picked in an income-based special enrollment period (the low-income SEP), for plan years after 2025 (sec. 71304); (2) remove the cap on repaying excess advance credits, from tax year 2026 (sec. 71305); (3) end credits for lawfully present immigrants under 100% FPL who can't get Medicaid because of status (sec. 71302). Separately, it bars credits for anyone cut from Medicaid for not meeting the work rule (A33).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-17"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q2, KFF's count of states where the A33 bar bites, and a scope note: credits are barred for people denied or cut solely for the work rule, in every state required to run it. KFF counts \"43 states and DC\", which matches T4-22's 44 including DC.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-18"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, EXPANSION STATES (KFF, published Oct 16, 2026): 41 including DC have adopted, 10 have not. NOT ADOPTED: Alabama, Florida, Georgia, Kansas, Mississippi, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Wisconsin, Wyoming (from the page's map data, quoted raw). Every other state plus DC is an expansion state, Indiana included (marked  for a trigger law). KFF's tracker lists no state that ended expansion or announced it would because of the rule. That is one source's tracker, so \"we found none\", not \"none exists\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-19"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, THE LAW'S REACH in the rule's words: the expansion adult group under the state plan, PLUS people 19–64 covered under a section 1115 waiver that gives minimum essential coverage (MEC) and who are not otherwise eligible under the state plan. A state with neither has NO applicable individuals. Limited-benefit waivers (family planning only, for example) are not MEC and are outside. Waivers can't switch the rule off, and CMS will not approve one that does. The rule's preamble still says \"40 States and the District of Columbia\", which comes from a December 2023 CMS map.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-20"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, CMS's OWN LIST of waiver populations (all-states call slides, June 9, 2026; read from Georgetown CCF's public copy because medicaid.gov refused). 13 populations in eight states: GA Pathways (and its ESI/HIPP); HI QUEST; MA MassHealth groups; NY (TANF); OR youth with special health care needs; TN TennCare III parent/caretaker relatives expansion; UT adult expansion and targeted adults; WI BadgerCare childless adults. So Georgia Pathways enrollees and Wisconsin's BadgerCare childless adults ARE covered by sec. 71119. (The slide text is OCR'd, hence \"W1 Badg&rcare\"; quoted as extracted.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-21"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, THE COUNT (KFF tracker, updated Sep 29, 2026): 44 including DC, meaning the 41 expansion states including DC plus three non-expansion states with listed waiver groups (Georgia, Tennessee, Wisconsin). For the viewers' states: Indiana, Minnesota, Pennsylvania, Illinois and Oregon are YES (expansion states). Georgia is YES, but only for Pathways enrollees. Wisconsin is YES for BadgerCare childless adults. Texas is NO: no expansion and no listed waiver group. This supersedes KFF's 2025 \"likely apply to Wisconsin and Georgia\". NOT checked: Minnesota's MinnesotaCare (a Basic Health Program, not Medicaid) and whether the rule touches it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-22"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, INDIANA, from its own agency (FSSA member page): HIP work requirements start January 1, 2027 for new applicants and members. Indiana takes the statute's MAXIMUM look-back for applicants, 3 months: a January applicant must have met it in October, November and December. Current members must show any 3 months since their last renewal. (The statute allows 1 to 3 months, A30; Nebraska uses 1.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-23"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, Indiana's context (Indiana Capital Chronicle, July 7, 2026; a press report, not the agency). HIP is Indiana's expansion. Enrollment fell from about 671,000 when the state law was signed (April 2025) to about 487,000 in June 2026, before any enforcement. The state's 10% share is paid through hospital assessment fees. Indiana's trigger law ends expansion if the federal MATCH RATE drops (T4-19 / E33). A work rule is not a match-rate cut, so the work rule on its own doesn't fire the trigger (our reading of the trigger's wording).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-24"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q3, WHO IS OUTSIDE IN EVERY STATE (cites T1-1, T1-5): only expansion adults (and listed waiver adults) can be subject, so every other Medicaid group is outside: children, people on SSI, parents in the parents-and-caretakers group, pregnant women. Under-19s and anyone on Medicare Part A or B are deemed compliant. One line: \"it applies only to adults 19 to 64 who got Medicaid through the expansion, or through one of a handful of named waivers\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-25"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, NEW and load-bearing: the $580 test is HOUSEHOLD income, and not only wages. The rule makes states use the person's MAGI-based income for their MAGI household. That includes countable UNEARNED income and the income of everyone in the household. A31's \"$580 at $7.25\" is right, but \"earn $580\" is the wrong verb.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-26"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, how Montana applies T4-26: a spouse's or housemate's income counts toward $580 when the household reaches it, but HOURS are personal. Nobody can count someone else's work, school or volunteer hours.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-27"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, how Indiana applies T4-26: ALL MAGI-counted income counts toward $580, taxable investment income and Social Security included. To turn pay into hours, Indiana divides GROSS income by $7.25 (its example: $507.50 = 70 hours). Self-employed people document hours or earnings with the latest tax return or other business records.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-28"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, GIG WORK COUNTS AS WORK: the rule says self-employment, owning a business and independent contracting all meet the definition of work. The $580 uses the FEDERAL minimum wage in every state, even where the state minimum is higher.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-29"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, GROSS OR NET for gig income? The rule says only \"MAGI-based income\" (T4-26). Under the tax-based MAGI rules, self-employment counts as NET profit after business expenses: the Schedule C figure, as HealthCare.gov puts it for the marketplace, which uses the same MAGI concept. So a driver's mileage and fees likely come off before the $580 test. Our inference: no community-engagement text says \"net\", and states may handle documents differently (Indiana's \"gross\" example in T4-28 concerns wages). Don't air a flat \"gross\" or \"net\" without that caveat.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-30"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, MONTANA'S ANSWER for the self-employed and gig workers: report income on the form; tax records may be asked for. If the household reaches $580 it is deemed compliant. If not, document HOURS, and gig platforms usually log both earnings and time. Seasonal workers can use the 6-month average.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-31"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, MONTANA'S MATRIX: employment is checked first against state labour-department wage data. Without a match: pay stubs, or tax returns for the self-employed and seasonal. Community service has NO data check; it needs Montana's verification form (Appendix A).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-32"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, Montana's member page on volunteering: it must be with a not-for-profit organisation, and signed volunteer logs are the named proof. (The federal rule is wider: public bodies also count, T4-34.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-33"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, WHAT COUNTS AS VOLUNTEERING (federal rule): a structured program run by a public or nonprofit body. States may NOT limit it to 501(c)(3) charities, so local government and church-run programs count. Court-ordered community service counts. Helping a friend move or doing a neighbour's yard work does NOT, unless it is done through an organisation that serves the community.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-34"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, WHAT PROOF A STATE MAY ASK FOR (federal rule). The rule's examples are pay stubs and a community-service organisation's document of hours. Through December 31, 2027 a state MAY accept other information (a statement) even when documents exist. From January 1, 2028 it must generally require documents when they are reasonably available. It must accept other information when none exist (a fire, a flood, informal caregiving). So \"does volunteering need an organisation's sign-off?\" depends on the state until 2028 and is generally yes after.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-35"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, NEBRASKA'S DIFFERENT CHOICE: its Declaration Form lets people DECLARE volunteering, education and work programs (plus some exemptions) without the organisation's paperwork. Paid work and income that data can't confirm still need primary-source documents (E10, E36). Applicants are tested on the month before they apply. Nebraska also states the seasonal rule: a 6-month average from seasonal work of $580 or more passes \"regardless of the number of hours\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-36"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, SEASONAL MECHANICS (federal rule; A31 says averaging is for seasonal workers only). States that use a \"reasonably predictable changes\" income method spread the season over up to 12 months. Others average the preceding 6 months. The rule's own example: a worker earning $1,500 a month from April to September PASSES at a December application ($750 average) under the 12-month method. Under the plain 6-month average the same worker applying in July FAILS ($500 < $580). Which method your state uses decides it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-37"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q4, Montana's renewal ask: if data can't confirm, members send Montana's forms and supporting documents and must show compliance (or an exclusion) in THREE months of the review period, not necessarily in a row. Nebraska asks for one month (E10). Different states, different counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t4-38"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Q1 steelman, both readings in one place (built on T4-6 to T4-9; no new source). \"Employers underpay and the public fills in\": in the six reporting states the largest national chains (Walmart, Amazon, Dollar General, McDonald's, Kroger) recur at the top. 17 of the companies that are top-25 in two or more states rank among the 50 largest employers in the Fortune 500 (T4-6). \"Low-wage jobs often lack affordable cover and Medicaid fills a real gap\": Medicaid workers are concentrated in small firms with no mandate to offer cover (T4-7, T4-8). They are less likely than other workers to be at 1,000+ firms. Part-timers are owed no offer even at big firms (T4-9). GAO's tables can't settle which reading is right (T4-10).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t4-39"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Nebraska, July–August renewals (E1, E4): the parts add up, and the comparators \"about one renewal in 14 lost coverage\", \"of every 12 who lost it, 11 hadn't answered\" and \"about six in a thousand were found not meeting the rule\". Working: 4,171 exempt + 2,449 qualified + 127 hardship = 6,747 approved; 6,747 + 533 lost = 7,280 ✓. 7,280 ÷ 533 = 13.66, \"about 1 in 14\". 490 ÷ 533 = 91.9%; 11 ÷ 12 = 91.7%, \"11 in 12\". 43 ÷ 7,280 = 0.59%, \"about six in a thousand\". 490 ÷ 7,280 = 6.7% (as E1).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w1"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"\"80 hours a month is a little under 20 hours a week.\" Working: 80 hours × 12 months ÷ 52 weeks = 18.5 hours a week. (The JAMA Health Forum study, D11, uses 20 hours a week as its threshold; it is that study's choice, not the rule's.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w2"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"\"The agency's own paperwork estimate works out to about four hours a year for each person asked to send something.\" Working: 35.2 million hours a year at renewal ÷ 8.8 million people who must provide information = 4.0 hours each (the rule's own line is \"x 2 responses/year\"); $454,784,000 ÷ 35.2 million hours = $12.92 an hour, matching the rule's rate. Applications are a separate 7.5 million hours (A20). Say \"the agency's own estimate\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w3"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"The peg hunt's draft description: \"every other state that expanded Medicaid has to start by January 1, 2027.\" Incomplete: a state that shows a good-faith effort can be exempted 6 months at a time, never past December 31, 2028, and the agency expects about 2 of about 10 requests to be approved (A22, A23, E45). The description now says \"unless the federal government grants it a delay\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w4"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"The peg hunt's draft description: Montana \"can deny or drop coverage for adults who can't show 80 hours a month of work, school or volunteering.\" Incomplete: monthly earnings of $580 count instead of hours (A31), Montana converts earnings below that into hours and lets people top up with other activities (E21), and exempt people aren't asked (E19). The description now says \"(or the earnings that count instead) and aren't exempt\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w5"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"The brief's vendor line: \"$600 million in pledges\" is \"not on the agency's page\" (from E38). Incomplete: E38 is right that the figure appears nowhere on the agency's pledges page, but the agency's own community-engagement page lists a press release whose TITLE reads \"Medicaid Technology Companies Pledge $600M in Savings…\", posted 1/29/2026 (found by hand pass T1 on its saved copy of that page). So the figure is the agency's own headline, not unsourced. The release's body was not opened, and the pledges page itemises terms (E38) that don't add to a total we can check. Right wording: \"the agency's own press release headlines $600 million in pledged savings; the itemised pledges don't show how it adds up\". Still off air as a figure until the release is read.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w6"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"SSA's clocks in viewer terms: \"about six months for a first disability decision, and well over a year more if you appeal\"; \"about as long as one six-month renewal\". Working: 185 days ÷ 30.4 = 6.1 months (T1-15). Appeal steps: 201 + 287 = 488 days ÷ 30.4 = 16.1 months. All three steps: 185 + 201 + 287 = 673 days ÷ 30.4 = 22.1 months (T1-15's \"≈ 670 days, about 22 months\"), decision time only, monthly averages for September 2026, with filing and mailing gaps on top. A six-month renewal (A29) is about 182 days, against 185. Label every total \"our sum of SSA's averages\", never \"SSA says\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w7"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Improper-enrolment comparators. (a) \"About 6% of Medicaid payments were improper in fiscal 2025; about three in four of those were missing paperwork; about one in six were for people found ineligible.\" Working: 6.12% (T2-1); 77.17% ≈ three-quarters (T2-1); $6.24B ÷ $37.39B = 16.7% ≈ one in six (CMS prints 16.70%, T2-3); $16.74B ÷ $37.39B = 44.8% (CMS 44.77%). (b) Expansion group 5.54% (CI 4.05–7.03) against all income-based groups 4.93% (CI 4.04–5.82): 0.61 points apart, intervals overlap (T2-4). (c) Paragon's range as shares: 9.2 million ÷ 0.46 = a base of about 20.0 million expansion enrollees; 5.2 million ÷ 20.0 = 26% (\"more than a quarter\", Paragon's own words); 11.5 million ÷ 20.0 = 57.5% (\"over half\"); 8.2 million ÷ 20.0 = 41% (T2-19). (d) GAO's cross-state duplicates: 149,000 ÷ 32.6 million Medicaid SSNs = 0.46%, about 1 in 220 in the six states (T2-11; people over SSNs, so a rough ratio: GAO itself says only \"relatively small\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w8"},{"s":"ep52-medicaid-work-requirements","n":52,"c":"Script check (2026-11-02, the ep52 script): \"in Nebraska, more than nine in ten renewals were approved, as exempt or qualified\" (Gold, s09against.c). Working: 4,171 exempt + 2,449 qualified + 127 hardship = 6,747 approved (W1); 6,747 ÷ 7,280 = 92.7%, \"more than nine in ten\". The approved count includes the 127 on a temporary hardship, so the spoken line says \"approved\", not \"met the rule\". Never \"verified automatically\" (E18): the state has published no ex parte share.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w9"}]);